| 1 | 3 | How media works on us. *How do the parts of a media piece build its message, and how does media touch our days?* | 14 | ma-g3-read; ma-g3-society | MA:Re7.1.3a; MA:Re7.1.3b; MA:Cn11.1.3a; MA:Cn11.1.3b | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11); creating practiced through viewfinder framing (review). Works: Ch�?j��-giga (Scrolls of Frolicking Animals), the first scroll (Japan, twelfth century, held by K�?zan-ji temple, Kyoto, and kept at the Tokyo and Kyoto National Museums; public domain, the image from a national museum's open page), animals racing and wrestling scene by scene along a handscroll read right to left; Shaun Tan, The Lost Thing (Australia, 2010, a short animated film he co-directed from his own picture book; Tan born 1974, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked to the maker's page; Curio Class's invented "day of media" cards (a school bell, a road sign, a cereal box, a game screen, a message on a phone, a bus poster), with no brand names | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 2's describing what a piece holds and says (ma-g2-read) and safe, fair tool use (ma-g2-society), with the ten-minute reteach. MA:Re7.1.3a: name the parts of a piece (frames or scenes, shot sizes, words, sound) and describe how they build its message, on the parts-and-message chart. MA:Re7.1.3b: name a technique or style (a close-up, a low angle, bright or dark color, quick or slow scene changes, music) and describe how it steers the viewer's experience. MA:Cn11.1.3a: name how media touches a day (the day-of-media cards), the values a piece asks us to hold (what it wants us to want or believe), and how people act online, on invented cases (a kind comment, an unkind one, a picture shared without asking). MA:Cn11.1.3b: examine the class's media tools and spaces (the viewfinder, a shared device, the screening corner) and use them with safety, fair play and the class rules, on the class tool and space card. Technique: framing with the card viewfinder at shot sizes (wide, medium, close-up) and angles (high, low, eye level). Words: parts, message, technique, style, audience, values. Showing: the class media map of the school (framed drawings or photographs of media in the building, no faces) on a hallway wall. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.3a, a written item on an unseen piece (six printed stills of a public domain film or a Curio Class original): three parts named and how they build its message; MA:Re7.1.3b, a talk item: one technique in an unseen still and how it steers the viewer; MA:Cn11.1.3a, a kept card made on the last making day: two day-of-media cards placed on the class chart with the value each asks for, and one invented online case sorted fair or unfair with a reason; MA:Cn11.1.3b, a table item: from a tray the student picks the right step for one tool or space (cord taped, volume low, ask before capturing anyone, device wiped and handed back) and says the rule. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: judging with criteria (unit 3), ethics and fantasy against reality (grade 4). Tools: the viewfinder step card, the parts-and-message chart, the day-of-media cards, the invented online cases and the tool and space card, all Curio Class's own; a work viewer on the teacher screen or printed stills. Free path: card viewfinders cut by an adult from cereal boxes. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | First because every later unit needs the class tool and space rules (asking before capturing anyone, safe and fair use) and the reading routine; the look-describe-interpret and media-ethics-check models return here from grade 2, where they are first needed this year. Reading how parts build a message comes before unit 2's plans and unit 3's purposeful order. 14 days: four ids, one technique and the room routine set up | Candidate: the bar's criterion 9 on device hand-out and hand-back minutes inside a 40-minute session | Online behavior is studied on invented cases only; no task asks about a student's own accounts, posts, games, screen time or what a family watches. A student who tells of harm online gets health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"). Ch�?j��-giga is held by a Buddhist temple: the unit uses the racing and wrestling scenes, not the scene that parodies a temple rite; the sensitivity agent reads the national museum's page first. Hazards and controls: viewfinders cut by an adult; device cords taped down or none; no capturing a person without their yes and, for a student, the family's yes. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | 3 | Ideas in motion. *How do media makers test an idea before they make it, and how does a crew share the work?* | 18 | ma-g3-ideas; ma-g3-craft | MA:Cr1.1.3; MA:Cr2.1.3; MA:Pr5.1.3a; MA:Pr5.1.3b; MA:Pr5.1.3c | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Moustapha Alassane, La Mort du Gandji (The Death of Gandji) (Niger, 1965; Alassane 1942 to 2015), a short animated fable with animal characters by a pioneer of animation in Africa, described and linked to a film archive's or festival's page (the unit plan confirms the film's page and summary); a zoetrope strip (England or the United States, 1860s to 1870s, public domain, a museum's or library's open collection; the unit plan picks the strip), twelve drawings that loop into motion when the drum spins | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 2's many ideas and a chosen sketch (ma-g2-ideas) and a role with named tool methods (ma-g2-craft), with the ten-minute reteach. MA:Cr1.1.3: develop several ideas for a 12-frame loop using different tools or materials (pencil, cut paper, found objects for sound). MA:Cr2.1.3: share and test the plan (a sketch strip or a four-frame mockup loop) with classmates before making, and note what the test changed. MA:Pr5.1.3a: show growing skill in a crew role (director, animator, sound maker composing the sound track, tool handler) and in group planning. MA:Pr5.1.3b: make up new content or answers inside the project (a new move for a character, a sound for a thing that has none). MA:Pr5.1.3c: use the tools in their standard ways (frames drawn in register on the strip's line, the flip book flipped at an even speed, a found-object sound played on the action's cue). Technique: the 12-frame loop in a flip book or paper zoetrope strip, with spacing (drawings close together for slow, far apart for fast), and live sound effects on cue. Words: develop, method, test, plan, role, tool handling. Showing: the crews' loops spun or flipped with their live sound for the class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Cr1.1.3, a guide look-for on the kept storyboard: at least three ideas, using two tools or materials; MA:Cr2.1.3, a guide look-for on the storyboard: a classmate's test and what it changed, in one line; MA:Pr5.1.3a, a kept card: the crew role record (role, two jobs done, one skill gained), with the teacher's look-for mark from the making days; MA:Pr5.1.3b, a guide look-for on the loop: one piece of content the student made up; MA:Pr5.1.3c, a table item: the student shows one tool's standard way (frames in register, an even flip, a sound on its cue). Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: an original goal and design steps (grade 4). Tools: the storyboard sheet, crew role cards, the loop strip template and the sound cue card, Curio Class's own; a frame-sequence viewer or the printed flip book. Free path: scrap paper, binder clips, found objects for sound. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | After unit 1 because the crew works by unit 1's tool and space rules and its framing. Longest in year 3: five ids, two techniques (the loop and live sound) and the crew roles; the storyboard and production-roles models return here from grade 2, where they are first needed. Before unit 3, whose purposeful order and stressed element build on a tested plan and a working crew | Candidate: a doubled parent-and-student crew for the home room (two roles each) | Alassane: the sensitivity agent reads a film archive's page and the maker's own published words first; the work card names Niger, the date and the maker. Crew roles rotate across the year, so no student is kept in one role. Hazards and controls: blunt-tip scissors; brass fasteners shown before use; strips spun by hand; no glass or sharp found objects for sound; sound kept soft, never near an ear; no loop drawn with alternating light and dark frames. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 3 | 3 | Order and stress. *How do the order of a piece and one stressed part change what it does, and how do we judge it fairly?* | 16 | ma-g3-make; ma-g3-judge | MA:Cr3.1.3a; MA:Cr3.1.3b; MA:Re8.1.3; MA:Re9.1.3 | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: A%tienne-Jules Marey, chronophotographs of a pelican in flight (France, about 1882, public domain, a museum's open collection), one movement broken into its stages; Juan Pablo Zaramella, Luminaris (Argentina, 2011; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a short film made a frame at a time with real people and objects, in which a worker in a light bulb factory carries out a plan of his own, described and linked to the maker's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 2's combined whole and a described change (ma-g2-make) and purpose, setting and ways to improve (ma-g2-judge), with the ten-minute reteach. MA:Cr3.1.3a: put varied content (frames, a title card, sound) in a purposeful order for a short piece (a 12-panel picture story or a 24-frame flip book), using movement and force (lines of action, spacing). MA:Cr3.1.3b: stress one element while finishing (a held frame, a close-up, a sudden sound) and analyze how it changes the effect and purpose, before and after, on the revision log. MA:Re8.1.3: judge what purposes and meanings a piece carries and describe its setting (who made it, where, when, for whom) from its work card. MA:Re9.1.3: name basic criteria (a clear order, a stressed part that works, sound that fits), evaluate a piece with them and consider one improvement with its setting in mind. Words: order, movement, force, stress, effect, criteria. Showing: the finished pieces at a class viewing, each beside its revision log. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Cr3.1.3a, a guide look-for on the finished piece: content in an order that serves its stated purpose, with movement or force shown; MA:Cr3.1.3b, a guide look-for on the revision log: the stressed element and one line analyzing its effect, before and after; MA:Re8.1.3, a written item on an unseen piece with its work card: its purpose, its meaning and one fact of its setting; MA:Re9.1.3, a talk item: two named criteria applied to an unseen piece and one improvement. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: balance and contrast, refining toward an intended effect (grade 4). Tools: the revision log, the criteria card, the critique card (describe, ask, suggest) and work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: scrap paper and pencil. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 2's tested plans and crew; the revision-log and critique-protocol models return here from grade 2, where a finished piece first needs them, and judging with named criteria for the first time needs a piece of the student's own to judge. Before unit 4, whose screening uses the ordered pieces and the criteria | Candidate: an ordered-motion work by a living maker from Africa or Asia, described and linked, for range | Critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class (row note). Zaramella's film shows factory work and a gentle act of rebellion; the sensitivity agent reads the maker's page. Hazards and controls: screen breaks, at most about ten minutes at a screen at a stretch; no flashing frames; blunt-tip scissors. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 4 | 3 | Our class screening. *How do we blend forms into one piece and share it, and how do popular retellings change what a story means?* | 12 | ma-g3-present; ma-g3-sources | MA:Pr4.1.3; MA:Pr6.1.3a; MA:Pr6.1.3b; MA:Cn10.1.3a; MA:Cn10.1.3b | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10); creating practiced (review). Works: Alanis Obomsawin, Sigwan (Canada, 2005, the National Film Board of Canada's free page at nfb.ca; Obomsawin is Abenaki, born 1932; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date, and confirms the film's page and summary), a short film for young viewers in which a girl is helped by the animals of the forest, described and linked; Georges MAcliA"s, Cendrillon (Cinderella) (France, 1899, public domain), an early popular film that told a fairy tale with stage tricks and added scenes of its own | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 2's unified piece and showing conditions (ma-g2-present) and what the student knows and likes as sources (ma-g2-sources), with the ten-minute reteach. MA:Pr4.1.3: practice blending forms and content (drawn or photographed frames, a narration, live sound, one fact from a school subject) into one unified piece: a narrated photo-story of six to eight frames. MA:Pr6.1.3a: describe the showing conditions (light, seats, sound level, the screen, box stage or wall) and take a named role and its steps for sharing (host, card puller or projectionist, sound crew, usher). MA:Pr6.1.3b: describe the screening's experience, share the results (what the audience noticed) and suggest one way to present better. MA:Cn10.1.3a: draw on the student's own interest and one outside source (a library book, a public domain picture or a Curio Class fact card), with an example, recorded on the source log; an invented or class-shared interest is marked the same. MA:Cn10.1.3b: show with an example how popular media shapes what a story means (what MAcliA"s's film added to Cinderella, set beside a class-invented retelling that changes it again). Words: blend, form, share, role, source, popular media. Showing: the class screening for another class or families, with each family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days; the screening is the first), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.3, a look-for marked at the screening: forms and content blended into one piece; MA:Pr6.1.3a, the kept screening-plan card (conditions described, the role's steps named), scored with a look-for on the role carried out at the screening; MA:Pr6.1.3b, a talk item: one result shared and one suggested improvement; MA:Cn10.1.3a, the kept source log: the interest, one outside source credited and how it was used; MA:Cn10.1.3b, a written item: one example of how a popular retelling changed what a story means. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a public sharing explained (grade 4). Tools: the screening-plan card, the source log and role cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: drawn frames on scrap paper, a box stage. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in year 3 because the screening uses the year's crew roles (unit 2) and ordered pieces and criteria (unit 3), and ends the year after the year-end check (week 26); the screening-plan and source-log models return here from grade 2. Shorter than unit 2 though it owns five ids, because frames and sounds kept from units 2 and 3 can be the piece's material | Candidate: the film board's page for Sigwan checked open and free on the day the unit plan is written | Obomsawin is Abenaki: the sensitivity agent reads the film board's page and the maker's own published words first; no student retells or imitates an Abenaki story. Sources: only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on (row note). No task asks what a family watches. Family yes for showing outside class and for any recording. Hazards and controls: the screening corner's cords taped down; sound at a comfortable level; no flashing. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 5 | 4 | Real or made? *How do media makers steer an audience, and how can we tell fantasy from reality?* | 14 | ma-g4-read; ma-g4-society | MA:Re7.1.4a; MA:Re7.1.4b; MA:Cn11.1.4a; MA:Cn11.1.4b | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11); creating practiced through angle and light (review). Works: Segundo de ChomA3n, El hotel elAcctrico (The Electric Hotel) (Spain, 1908, public domain), a trick film in which luggage unpacks itself and brushes move alone, made a frame at a time; AA_da Muluneh, staged color photographs from the series The World is 9 (Ethiopia, 2016; born 1974; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date and picks the photographs), described and linked to the artist's or a museum's page; Curio Class's invented "real or made?" picture pairs (an invented scene photographed and the same scene changed) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 3's parts and message (ma-g3-read, unit 1) and the tool and space card (ma-g3-society, unit 1). MA:Re7.1.4a: name and describe a piece's parts, then explain how they build its message. MA:Re7.1.4b: name and describe techniques and styles (angle, light, color, speed, music), then explain how they steer the audience. MA:Cn11.1.4a: explain, in words or in a short media piece (three framed pictures with captions), how media ties to culture, such as fantasy against reality: how a trick film makes the impossible look real, and how a changed picture can be taken for a real one. MA:Cn11.1.4b: examine the class's tools and spaces and use them with ethics and fair play, on the ethics card (ask before you capture, never change a picture of a real person, credit what you use). Technique: angle and light for effect (high, low and eye level; front light and side light from a flashlight or a window). Words: explain, technique, style, fantasy, reality, ethics. Showing: a "real or made?" wall for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.4a, a written item on unseen stills: two parts and how they build the message, explained; MA:Re7.1.4b, a talk item: one technique in an unseen still and why it steers the viewer; MA:Cn11.1.4a, a guide look-for on the kept three-picture piece or written explanation: fantasy told from reality, with the trick named; MA:Cn11.1.4b, a table item: an ethics-card case sorted fair or not, with the rule named. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: online spaces (unit 8), advertising and media literacy (grade 5). Tools: the ethics card, the real-or-made pairs and the angle and light step card, Curio Class's own. Free path: a flashlight or window light, the card viewfinder. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | First in year 4 because it reopens the tool rules (now with ethics) and the reading routine after the summer, and moves reading from describing how to explaining why. The fantasy-against-reality study needs the angle and light taught here, and unit 6's tool used in a new way builds on it | Candidate: a fantasy work from the Americas by a living maker, described and linked, for range | No changed picture of a real person, classmate or teacher is made or shown; the real-or-made pairs use invented subjects only. Muluneh's photographs draw on Ethiopian symbols and dress: the sensitivity agent reads the artist's own words and a museum's page first; students study the photographs and do not imitate the dress. Hazards and controls: flashlights low-power, never shone in eyes; no laser pointers; device cords taped down or none. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 6 | 4 | Design it, test it. *How does a crew turn a goal into a working plan, and how can a tool be used in a new way?* | 18 | ma-g4-ideas; ma-g4-craft | MA:Cr1.1.4; MA:Cr2.1.4; MA:Pr5.1.4a; MA:Pr5.1.4b; MA:Pr5.1.4c | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Nobur�? �Ofuji, a cut-paper animation made with chiyogami, Japanese patterned paper (Japan, 1920s to 1950s; �Ofuji 1900 to 1961; the unit plan picks the film and records its rights line), described and linked; Joseph Plateau, phenakistiscope discs (Belgium, 1833, public domain, a museum's open collection), a disc of drawings that moves when spun before a mirror | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 3's tested plans (ma-g3-ideas, unit 2) and crew roles (ma-g3-craft, unit 2). MA:Cr1.1.4: set an original goal for a stop-motion piece with sketches and mockups. MA:Cr2.1.4: discuss, test and combine two plans and models while keeping the goal and the showing in mind. MA:Pr5.1.4a: rotate crew roles that build technique, equipment use, production and teamwork (animator, capture, set and props, sound, director). MA:Pr5.1.4b: solve a media problem with design steps (define, sketch, test, change). MA:Pr5.1.4c: use a tool in its usual way and a new way (the viewfinder as a capture frame, a spinning disc drawn as a moving title, a flashlight as a moving light). Technique: paper cut-out stop-motion with replacement parts (move a little, capture, move again), on a shared device or as a drawn frame strip. Words: goal, mockup, combine, design thinking, equipment, new way. Showing: the crews' stop-motion clips or frame strips shown to the class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Cr1.1.4, a guide look-for on the kept storyboard: an original goal stated, with sketches and a mockup; MA:Cr2.1.4, a guide look-for on the storyboard: two plans combined, with the goal kept; MA:Pr5.1.4a, a kept card: the role record, two roles taken in rotation and one skill each; MA:Pr5.1.4b, a kept card: the design card (define, sketch, test, change), with one change made after a test; MA:Pr5.1.4c, a table item: one tool shown in its usual way and in a new way. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a convention bent on purpose, experimental against ordinary use (grade 5). Tools: the storyboard sheet, the design card, role cards and the stop-motion step card, Curio Class's own; a frame-sequence viewer or the printed frame strip. Free path: drawn frame strips, scrap card, brass fasteners. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | After unit 5 because a new use of a tool grows from angle and light, and the crew needs the ethics card. Longest in year 4: five ids, the stop-motion technique and the rotating roles. Before unit 7, whose arranged and refined piece uses the crew's capture skills | Candidate: a cut-paper or stop-motion maker from Africa or the Americas, living, described and linked | Hazards and controls: a craft knife only in an adult's hand; blunt-tip scissors; the device on a stand or taped down; screen breaks; discs spun by hand, with no alternating light and dark frames. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 7 | 4 | Balance and contrast. *How do makers arrange a piece for the effect they want, and how do we judge each step?* | 14 | ma-g4-make; ma-g4-judge | MA:Cr3.1.4a; MA:Cr3.1.4b; MA:Re8.1.4; MA:Re9.1.4 | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Graciela Iturbide, Nuestra SeA�ora de las Iguanas (Our Lady of the Iguanas) (JuchitA�n, Oaxaca, Mexico, 1979; born 1942; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a woman at market with live iguanas on her head, described and linked to a museum's page; Ansel Adams, The Tetons and the Snake River (Grand Teton National Park, Wyoming, United States, 1942, made for a federal mural project, the National Archives, public domain), light and dark set in strong contrast | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 3's ordered piece and stressed element (ma-g3-make, unit 3) and named criteria (ma-g3-judge, unit 3). MA:Cr3.1.4a: arrange varied content (photographs or drawn frames, captions, sound) to carry a stated purpose, using balance and contrast: a six-frame photo-story. MA:Cr3.1.4b: refine the piece toward the intended effect by stressing chosen elements, logged on the revision log. MA:Re8.1.4: decide and explain a reaction to different pieces, weighing purpose and context. MA:Re9.1.4: name and use basic criteria to judge and improve pieces and a production step (a storyboard, a capture session, a sound take). Words: arrange, balance, contrast, refine, reaction, context. Showing: the photo-stories at a class viewing. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Cr3.1.4a, a guide look-for on the finished piece: content arranged for a stated purpose, with balance or contrast; MA:Cr3.1.4b, a guide look-for on the revision log: two refinements toward the intended effect, each naming what was stressed; MA:Re8.1.4, a written item on an unseen piece with its work card: a reasoned reaction naming its purpose and one fact of its context; MA:Re9.1.4, a talk item: two criteria applied to a production step (a classmate's storyboard, shared with permission) and one improvement. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: emphasis and refining for clarity (grade 5). Tools: the revision log, the criteria card, the critique card and work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: drawn frames, pencil shading for contrast. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 6's capture skills and crew; the revision log and critique card return from year 3 one step harder (criteria on production steps). Before unit 8, whose public showing uses a piece arranged for an effect and the habit of judging steps | Candidate: a balance-and-contrast work from Asia or Africa, open access, for range | Iturbide's photograph shows a Zapotec woman of JuchitA�n: the sensitivity agent reads the artist's own words and a JuchitA�n or Zapotec source first; students study the photograph and do not imitate the dress. The work card for the Tetons names, in one line, the Shoshone and other peoples whose homelands the park is on, from the park's own page. Critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class. Hazards and controls: screen breaks; device cords taped down or none. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 8 | 4 | Show it to the school. *How do we join a school subject, an art form and media in one piece, and how do online spaces make meaning?* | 14 | ma-g4-present; ma-g4-sources | MA:Pr4.1.4; MA:Pr6.1.4a; MA:Pr6.1.4b; MA:Cn10.1.4a; MA:Cn10.1.4b | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10); creating practiced (review). Works: Skawennati, CyberPowWow (1997 to 2004; Skawennati is Kanien'kehA�:ka (Mohawk) from KahnawA�:ke; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), an online gallery and meeting place built by Native artists, described and linked to the artist's own page; Jean PainlevAc, L'Hippocampe (The Seahorse) (France, 1934), a science film of seahorses joined with music, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 3's blended piece and showing role (ma-g3-present, unit 4) and the source log (ma-g3-sources, unit 4). MA:Pr4.1.4: show how forms from a school subject, an art form and media can be coordinated in one piece (a science or social studies topic the student researched, a drawing, a tune or a movement, and a stop-motion clip, photo-story or sound piece). MA:Pr6.1.4a: explain the showing conditions for a public sharing in the school (a hallway screening or a family evening) and handle a role and its steps. MA:Pr6.1.4b: explain how the showing went and what to improve. MA:Cn10.1.4a: look into an interest, research it with two outside sources, and add cultural knowledge (the student's own, or a public or invented one, marked the same) to inform the piece, on the source log. MA:Cn10.1.4b: show how media makes meaning and cultural experience in online spaces (CyberPowWow as a space Native artists built for themselves; a Curio Class invented online space on printed screens). Words: coordinate, public, explain, research, cultural knowledge, online. Showing: the public school showing, with each family's yes. End-of-unit evidence (two check days; the showing is the first), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.4, a look-for marked at the showing: subject, art form and media coordinated; MA:Pr6.1.4a, the kept screening-plan card (conditions explained, the role's steps), scored with a look-for on the role carried out; MA:Pr6.1.4b, a talk item: how the showing went and one improvement; MA:Cn10.1.4a, the kept source log: two sources credited and one line on what each informed; MA:Cn10.1.4b, a written item: one way an online space makes meaning or a shared experience, with the example. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: two formats compared (grade 5). Tools: the screening-plan card, the source log, the invented online space screens and role cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: drawn frames, a box stage, a printed showing program. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in year 4 because the public showing uses the year's stop-motion and crew (unit 6) and an arranged piece (unit 7), and ends the year after the year-end check (week 25); the screening plan and source log return from year 3 one step harder (a public showing, research) | Candidate: the school subject topics drawn from what the grade's science and social studies courses teach that term, listed on a topic card | Skawennati: the sensitivity agent reads the artist's own pages and a KahnawA�:ke community source first; no student imitates Kanien'kehA�:ka designs or dress; CyberPowWow is studied by description and the still images on the artist's page, and no student goes online in class. Cultural knowledge is optional, with a public or invented topic marked the same, and no student is asked to explain their family's culture. Family yes for the public showing and any recording. Hazards and controls: a clear floor if a movement is joined; cords taped down; sound at a comfortable level. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 9 | 5 | Selling and telling. *How do two pieces steer us differently, and how has advertising shaped a community over time?* | 16 | ma-g5-read; ma-g5-society | MA:Re7.1.5a; MA:Re7.1.5b; MA:Cn11.1.5a; MA:Cn11.1.5b | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11); creating practiced through a poster's eye path (review). Works: advertising trade cards (United States, 1870s to 1890s, public domain, a public library's open digital collection), small picture cards that shops handed out; the unit plan picks cards free of the racial and ethnic caricatures common in this form; hand-painted film posters from Ghana's traveling video shows (Ghana, about 1985 to the 2000s; painters such as Daniel Anum Jasper; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date, and picks a poster free of violence or horror), described and linked to a museum's or exhibition's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 4's explained parts and techniques (ma-g4-read, unit 5) and the ethics card (ma-g4-society, unit 5). MA:Re7.1.5a: name, describe and tell apart how the parts of two pieces build message and meaning (a trade card beside a painted poster). MA:Re7.1.5b: name, describe and tell apart how their techniques and styles steer the audience. MA:Cn11.1.5a: investigate how media ties into life and community, including advertising, its history and its ethics (what an ad leaves out; who is shown and who is not), in a short piece: an ad for an invented product with a caption on what it leaves out. MA:Cn11.1.5b: examine and discuss the class's tools and spaces and keep the class checklist for ethical, media-literate use (who made this, why, what is left out, may I use it). Technique: a poster or title card with emphasis by size, color and placement (the eye path). Words: tell apart, steer, advertising, history, ethics, media literacy. Showing: the invented-ad wall with its "what it leaves out" captions. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.5a, a written item on two unseen pieces: how their parts build different messages; MA:Re7.1.5b, a talk item: one technique in each and how they steer differently; MA:Cn11.1.5a, a guide look-for on the kept ad and caption: one ad technique used, what it leaves out, and one fact of advertising's history from the work cards; MA:Cn11.1.5b, a kept card: the class checklist applied to one unseen piece. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: news coverage (unit 12). Tools: the media-literacy checklist, the eye-path step card and work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: scrap card and markers. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in year 5 because it reopens the reading routine and the tool rules (now media literacy) after the summer, and moves reading to two pieces told apart. 16 days, two more than years 3 and 4, because the history of advertising adds a study with its own work cards. Unit 12's news study builds on its checklist | Candidate: an advertising work from Asia, open access, for range | Trade cards: many carry caricatures of African American, Chinese American, Irish American and Native people; the sensitivity agent checks each card picked and the library's page; if a student asks why such cards existed, the teacher names the harm plainly in one line from the library's own words and moves on. Ghana posters: the sensitivity agent reads the museum's or exhibition's page and the painters' own published words first. Ads are for invented products only, with no brand names; no task asks what a family buys or wants. Hazards and controls: markers labeled non-toxic. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 10 | 5 | Bend the rules. *How do makers draw ideas from other artists' work, and what happens when a tool is used in an experimental way?* | 18 | ma-g5-ideas; ma-g5-craft | MA:Cr1.1.5; MA:Cr2.1.5; MA:Pr5.1.5a; MA:Pr5.1.5b; MA:Pr5.1.5c | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Te Wei with a team of animators, Where Is Mama (Xiao kedou zhao mama) (Shanghai, China, 1960), an animated film made in the style of ink-wash painting, tadpoles looking for their mother, described and linked; PES (Adam Pesapane), Western Spaghetti (United States, 2008; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), object stop-motion in which everyday things stand in for food, described and linked to the maker's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 4's goal and combined plans (ma-g4-ideas, unit 6) and rotating roles and design steps (ma-g4-craft, unit 6). MA:Cr1.1.5: imagine new ideas from the student's own experience or another artist's work (the two films), credited. MA:Cr2.1.5: develop and test a proposal and a model (a short test strip or clip) with the goal and a stated audience in mind (a grade 2 class, families). MA:Pr5.1.5a: practice varied roles that build skills in art, design, technology and teamwork. MA:Pr5.1.5b: bend a usual convention on purpose to solve a problem in the project (an object standing in for another, a sound that does not match the picture on purpose). MA:Pr5.1.5c: study ordinary and experimental ways a tool can serve (a brush for outlines and a brush for wash; the viewfinder or device held still and moved). Technique: object stop-motion with stand-in objects, and wash animation on paper (each frame painted in thin washable ink or watercolor). Words: imagine, proposal, audience, convention, ordinary, experimental. Showing: the test films or strips for the stated audience. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Cr1.1.5, a guide look-for on the kept storyboard: ideas drawn from the student's experience or a named artist's work, credited; MA:Cr2.1.5, a guide look-for on the storyboard: a proposal naming the audience, and a model tested with its result; MA:Pr5.1.5a, a kept card: the role record, two roles and one skill each in art, design, technology or teamwork; MA:Pr5.1.5b, a guide look-for on the piece: a convention bent on purpose, named in the student's line; MA:Pr5.1.5c, a written item: one tool's ordinary and experimental use compared. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: an assigned role and tested constraints (grade 6). Tools: the storyboard sheet, the proposal card, role cards and the object stop-motion step card, Curio Class's own. Free path: found objects, drawn frame strips, water and a brush. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | After unit 9 because a proposal for an audience uses its reading of how pieces steer an audience. Longest in year 5: five ids, two techniques and the varied roles. Before unit 11, whose emphasis and clarity work refines a piece the crew can now capture | Candidate: an experimental animation by a living maker from Africa or Latin America, described and linked | Ideas drawn from another artist's work are credited by name. Hazards and controls: no food, glass or sharp objects as stop-motion objects (allergies, waste, cuts); ink and paint labeled non-toxic and washable; hands washed; water cups on a tray; the device on a stand. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 11 | 5 | Make it clear. *How does emphasis make a meaning clear, and how do my reading and the group's compare?* | 12 | ma-g5-make; ma-g5-judge | MA:Cr3.1.5a; MA:Cr3.1.5b; MA:Re8.1.5; MA:Re9.1.5 | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Buster Keaton and Edward F. Cline, One Week (United States, 1920, public domain), a silent comedy in which a newly married couple build a kit house that goes wrong; Abbas Kiarostami, Bread and Alley (Nan va kucheh) (Tehran, Iran, 1970; Kiarostami 1940 to 2016), a short film of a boy carrying bread home past a dog in an alley, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 4's arranged and refined piece (ma-g4-make, unit 7) and criteria on production steps (ma-g4-judge, unit 7). MA:Cr3.1.5a: make content and join its components (shots, title cards, sound) to express a meaning, using emphasis (a cut-in close-up, a held shot, a sound cue). MA:Cr3.1.5b: decide how elements could change for clearer communication, then refine for clarity (the revision log names the decision before the change). MA:Re8.1.5: compare the student's reading of a piece with the group's, weighing its aim and context. MA:Re9.1.5: set criteria and use them to evaluate pieces and production steps, giving kind, useful feedback that names a look-for. Technique: the cut (shot order, the cut-in close-up, continuity) in a six- to ten-shot sequence, drawn on a shot strip and cut and reordered with scissors and tape, or captured on a shared device. Words: join, emphasis, clarity, refine, compare, feedback. Showing: the sequences at a class viewing. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Cr3.1.5a, a guide look-for on the finished sequence: components joined to a stated meaning, with emphasis; MA:Cr3.1.5b, a guide look-for on the revision log: the decision for clarity written before the refinement, and the change made; MA:Re8.1.5, a written item on an unseen piece: the student's reading beside the group's (from the class chart), with its aim and one fact of its context; MA:Re9.1.5, a kept card: the student's own criteria and written feedback to a classmate that names a look-for. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: several approaches tried for a point of view (grade 6). Tools: the shot-strip template, the revision log, the criteria card and the feedback card, Curio Class's own; a shot-strip editor or the printed strip. Free path: scrap paper, scissors and tape. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | After unit 10's crew and capture skills; the revision log and critique card return one step harder (the change decided first; the student's own criteria). Before unit 12, whose newscast needs clear emphasis and the habit of comparing readings | Candidate: the group-reading chart tried as a paper version for one adult at home | Keaton's comedy uses falls and stunts: no student re-enacts a stunt or a fall. Kiarostami's film has a barking dog that frightens the boy: a student afraid of dogs is told before viewing and may study stills instead. Critique talks about the work, never the maker. Hazards and controls: blunt-tip scissors; screen breaks. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 12 | 5 | Our newscast. *How do news and community events shape what people remember, and which format tells our story best?* | 14 | ma-g5-present; ma-g5-sources | MA:Pr4.1.5; MA:Pr6.1.5a; MA:Pr6.1.5b; MA:Cn10.1.5a; MA:Cn10.1.5b | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10); creating practiced (review). Works: JosAc Guadalupe Posada, a news broadside (Mexico City, 1890s to 1910s, public domain, the Library of Congress or a museum's open collection; the unit plan picks a sheet on a non-violent event, not a calavera); a Lakota winter count from the Smithsonian's online exhibit "Lakota Winter Counts" (National Anthropological Archives; the unit plan picks the count), in which each year's main event is kept as one picture; a newsreel story of a community event (United States, 1930s to 1960s, the National Archives, public domain; the unit plan picks a fair, a parade or a school event, never a disaster or a war) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 4's coordinated piece and public showing (ma-g4-present, unit 8) and research with sources (ma-g4-sources, unit 8). MA:Pr4.1.5: make a piece that blends different kinds of content: a class newscast (an anchor, a report, an interview, a title card or a picture chart). MA:Pr6.1.5a: weigh what two showing formats offer (a live newscast in a box frame before an audience, against a recorded take or a printed news sheet) and fulfill a role in presenting. MA:Pr6.1.5b: compare the two formats' results and ways to improve. MA:Cn10.1.5a: gather and use the student's knowledge, interests, experiences and outside sources (a school event, an interview with a consenting adult at school, a library source), on the source log; a public or invented story is marked the same. MA:Cn10.1.5b: show how news coverage or a community event shapes people's cultural experience (what a broadside, a winter count and a newsreel each kept of an event, and what people remember because of it). Words: newscast, format, compare, gather, news, community event. Showing: the newscast for another class or families, with each family's yes. End-of-unit evidence (two check days; the newscast is the first), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.5, a look-for marked at the showing: kinds of content blended into one newscast; MA:Pr6.1.5a, the kept screening-plan card (two formats weighed), scored with a look-for on the role carried out; MA:Pr6.1.5b, a talk item: the two formats' results compared and one improvement; MA:Cn10.1.5a, the kept source log: the sources gathered, credited and used; MA:Cn10.1.5b, a written item: how one of the three works shaped what people remember of an event. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: formats analyzed against a core message, with a written review (grade 6). Tools: the screening-plan card, the source log, the newscast run sheet and role cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: a box frame, a printed news sheet. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in the band because the newscast blends the year's reading of ads and news (unit 9), its crew and capture (unit 10) and its emphasis for clarity (unit 11), and ends the year after the year-end check (week 25); the source thread then goes to grade 6's weighed sources and history | Candidate: the winter count's source confirmed as made with Lakota keepers, from the exhibit's own credits | Lakota winter count: the sensitivity agent first studies the Smithsonian exhibit, made with Lakota keepers, and a Lakota-run source; it is studied by looking only; no student makes a winter count or uses Lakota pictographs, and the class's own year record uses its own form. Posada: no calaveras, crime or execution sheets. The newscast reports school or invented events; no student reports on a classmate's family or a private matter; interviews are with consenting adults at school, recorded only with the family's yes; a student may take an off-camera role (sound, picture chart, floor manager), and the printed news sheet keeps every id met (leave-out line). Hazards and controls: cords taped down; sound at a comfortable level; no flashing. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |

## Course-level checks

| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Year 3 mid-year (week 17, days 33 and 34) | 2 | 1 | 1 | Samples unit 1 in its own year and carries unit 2: a written item on an unseen piece, three parts and how they build its message (MA:Re7.1.3a), a talk item on how a technique in a new still steers the viewer (MA:Re7.1.3b), and a fresh table item at the teacher's table, one tool or space step from a new tray (MA:Cn11.1.3b). Unit 2's storyboard and role record (days 30 and 31) are carried unscored; no loop task form repeats. Respond on day 34. |
| Year 3 year-end (week 26, days 51 and 52) | 3 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 1 to 3: a fresh two-idea plan for a new prompt tested with a partner (MA:Cr1.1.3, MA:Cr2.1.3, unit 2), a written item on an unseen piece naming its purpose and one fact of its setting (MA:Re8.1.3, unit 3), and a talk item placing a new day-of-media card and the value it asks for (MA:Cn11.1.3a, unit 1). Unit 3's piece and revision log (days 47 and 48) are carried unscored. Unit 4 is assessed by its own screening. Respond on day 52. |
| Year 4 mid-year (week 17, days 97 and 98) | 6 | 1 | 1 | Samples unit 5 in its own year and carries unit 6: a written item explaining how two parts of unseen stills build the message (MA:Re7.1.4a), a talk item on why a technique in a new still steers the viewer (MA:Re7.1.4b), and a fresh ethics-card case sorted at the teacher's table (MA:Cn11.1.4b). Unit 6's storyboard and design card (days 94 and 95) are carried unscored. Respond on day 98. |
| Year 4 year-end (week 25, days 113 and 114) | 7 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 5 to 7: a fresh goal and two quick plans combined for a new prompt (MA:Cr1.1.4, MA:Cr2.1.4, unit 6), a written item giving a reasoned reaction to an unseen piece with its purpose and context (MA:Re8.1.4, unit 7), and a talk item telling fantasy from reality in a new real-or-made pair (MA:Cn11.1.4a, unit 5). Unit 7's piece and revision log (days 109 and 110) are carried unscored. Unit 8 is assessed by its own showing. Respond on day 114. |
| Year 5 mid-year (week 18, days 163 and 164) | 10 | 1 | 1 | Samples unit 9 in its own year and carries unit 10: a written item telling apart how the parts of two unseen pieces build their messages (MA:Re7.1.5a), a talk item on how one technique in each steers differently (MA:Re7.1.5b), and a fresh checklist case at the teacher's table (MA:Cn11.1.5b). Unit 10's storyboard and role record (days 160 and 161) are carried unscored. Respond on day 164. |
| Year 5 year-end (week 25, days 177 and 178) | 11 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 9 to 11: a fresh proposal for a new audience with one test named (MA:Cr2.1.5, unit 10), a written item comparing the student's reading of an unseen piece with a given group reading, with its aim and context (MA:Re8.1.5, unit 11), and a talk item on what a new invented ad leaves out (MA:Cn11.1.5a, unit 9). Unit 11's sequence and revision log (days 174 and 175) are carried unscored. Unit 12 is assessed by its own showing. Respond on day 178. |

Each check day follows the talk-and-written plan in Scored evidence: set-up, self-check, the written item for the
whole class, six groups of five at the teacher's table, clean-up, inside the 40 minutes. Every student's evidence is
collected once in a window; no artifact is scored twice. Days above are band course days (year 4 starts on day 65,
year 5 on day 129).

## Cross-subject

One row carries a cross_subject lean: ma-g3-judge leans on ela:SL.3.1 (class talk about what pieces mean and whether
they work). The other contacts below are practiced, not owned, each with its "if later" line; all are core courses
(lesson 32).

### English language arts grade 3 (same year)

| Used here | Lean | Earliest unit of English language arts grade 3 that teaches it | If ELA has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|---|
| Unit 3 (year 3, course days 35 to 50, weeks 18 to 25): the critique and the criteria talk (ma-g3-judge) | SL.3.1, collaborative discussion | Unit 1 (days 1 to 39), well before this unit | The critique card shows the discussion rules (come prepared with your notes, listen, build on what was said, ask a question) and the teacher models them on the first critique day |

### English language arts grades 4 and 5 (same year)

Written items and critiques practice short claim-and-reason writing (W.4.1, W.5.1) and class talk (SL.4.1, SL.5.1):
practiced, not owned. If ELA has not yet taught a reason with evidence, the written item's frame ("I think ___
because in the piece ___.") carries it.

### Mathematics, grades 3 to 5

No unit asks for rates, fractions, scale or measuring. Frames and panels are counted, never divided into seconds;
the year 5 newscast's run sheet gives each segment's length as minutes read from the classroom clock, which grade 3
math teaches (telling time to the minute), so no unit uses math ahead of math (lesson 13).

### Science and social studies, grades 3 to 5

Unit 8's coordinated piece draws its school-subject content from a topic the student chooses from what the grade's
science or social studies course has taught, on a topic card; nothing waits on a set unit, and the source cards
carry the facts used. Unit 12's broadside, winter count and newsreel are taught with their setting on work cards (bar
criterion 7), not leaned on social studies.

### Computer science

STANDARD.md 6: media arts teaches the one idea of privacy or copyright its unit needs (the tool and space card, the
ethics card, the media-literacy checklist, the source log) in a short step of the unit and does not wait for
computer science, which is not core; the deeper unit is computer-science:g8-attribution.

## For the lead

1. **Media arts connecting ids swapped (settled), as in the K to 2 frame.** Checked at source (the California standards page for
   MA:Cn10 grade 3): anchor 10 is the student's own experience and anchor 11 society, culture and history, as in
   every other discipline. The words-file lines for MA:Cn10.1.* and MA:Cn11.1.* were swapped, every ma-*-sources
   row now carries the Cn10 ids and every ma-*-society row the Cn11 ids, and this frame's id labels changed to match
   (the pairs of rows stay in their units).
2. **After-core check weeks.** Weeks chosen from the core calendar and the units' load: year 3 weeks 17 and 26, year
   4 weeks 17 and 25, year 5 weeks 18 and 25; the once-a-week respond sessions fall in weeks 18 and 27 (year 3), 18
   and 26 (year 4), 19 and 26 (year 5). Grade 3's year-end has only week 26 free of core checks with a free week
   after it (25 and 28 are core weeks). `frame.py calendar` shows after-core clashes in grade 3 weeks 17 and 26, grade 4 weeks 17 and 25 and grade 5
   week 18 (dance, theater, visual arts, physical education, social and emotional learning); left for the lead's one
   calendar pass.
3. **Media arts weekly time figure.** STANDARD.md 3.4 has no media arts row; the visual and media arts association's
   elementary checklist (arteducators.org, "OTL-Elementary_Visual_Media_Arts_Program.pdf", the file the visual arts
   pilot read) is read at source for its media arts lines, then STANDARD.md gains a "| Media arts, 3 to 5 |" row and
   the Pending note in the Days bullet is replaced with the figure and both paths' gaps. A download, so not done here.
4. **Paper path meets the tool, capture and online ids (item-level, decided, as the K to 2 frame).** A media tool
   here is anything that frames, captures, orders, plays or shows pictures and sound, so a device-free class meets
   MA:Pr5.1.3a to 5c, the Cn11 tool-and-space ids and the online ids in full on paper and printed invented screens.
   If the lead or Cory wants capture or editing met only on a device, the course needs one shared device per group
   of five, a cost question.
5. **Work picks the unit plans must confirm on the item's page:** the summary of Obomsawin's Sigwan (unit 4), the
   film and page for Alassane's La Mort du Gandji (unit 2), which �Ofuji film (unit 6), and the living status of the
   Ghanaian poster painters (unit 9). Each is named as a pick with "the unit plan confirms"; a swap keeps the ids and
   the band's range.
6. **Supplies ceiling (money, for Cory).** Provisional costs: $45 (year 3), $50 (year 4), $50 (year 5) a class of 30,
   all optional beside a free path. Pick: $50 a class a year for grades 3 to 5 media arts, like science K to 5's
   settled $50 ceiling, with every unit's free path kept.
