212: ### 2. Arts: visual arts, music, theater, dance, media arts
213: 
214: Twenty courses: five disciplines, four bands.
215: 
216: **Rests on.** Texas fine arts rubric (2025) and California's five categories, read in full by the writer;
217: Oklahoma's rubric (2024; four gateways, the fourth on state law), read in full by the expert.
218: 
219: 1. [I] All four artistic processes are in each course: create; perform, present or produce; respond; connect.
220: 2. [I] Technique is taught directly, in steps, and gets harder.
221: 3. [I] The discipline's words are taught and used in making and responding.
222: 4. [I] Students make, adapt or extend their own work and show it, with some choice in what they make or study.
223: 5. [I] Students are taught to read, judge and respond to other artists' work.
224: 6. [I] Students give and get feedback, and reflect to refine their work.
225: 7. [I] Works and traditions come from many times and cultures, with history and setting taught.
226: 8. [I] Assessment ranges across the year: performances and finished works scored with a guide, self-assessment, and
227:    work kept over time.
228: 9. [I] Pacing fits real arts schedules (rotations, short weeks). Lessons list equipment and safe use of space.
229: 
230: **Fails if:** prompts to express with no technique taught; no performing or showing; response work without the
231: discipline's words; one culture's canon; a plan that needs a daily period.
232: 
233: **Unsupported.** No rubric treats the five disciplines apart or says how to choose works. A screen cannot teach a
234: performing art, so the performing is in the room.
235: 
236: **Standards.** data/standards/ncas-performance-standards.txt (2014; a national revision is under way, no date). The
237: standards are by grade to grade 8; our courses are bands.
238: 
239: **Scope and sequence states:** which grade's standards fall in which year of the band. **Unit plan fills (field 2):** the
240: technique, the works studied and the showing. **Confidence:** likely for the instruments, thin for the evidence.
241: 
