194: ## After-core courses (added 2026-11-25)
195: The after-core subjects (arts, health, physical education, computer science, career education, SEL, ELD, library)
196: use this brief with these changes. Their own pages outrank the core lessons above where they differ.
197: 
198: - **Read first, in place of items 2, 6 and 7:** the subject's docs/courses/<subject>/STANDARD.md and APPROACH.md in
199:   full, docs/agents/lenses/<subject>.md, the subject's row in docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md ("Before a scope and
200:   sequence in this subject") and its bar part, every DESK-ANSWERS.md entry that names the subject, and the
201:   subject's stage 1 and progression reviews (docs/reviews/prework-<subject>-*, progression-<subject>-*). The core
202:   frames the course leans on are read at their Units tables only (docs/courses/<core course>/frame.md).
203: - **The rows:** the course's `rows` cell in data/plans/course-list.csv selects them (`file:` the progression file,
204:   `grade:` the grades, `strand:` the strand, `|` between values). `python -X utf8 scripts/frame.py check <slug>`
205:   checks every one is placed once.
206: - **Band courses** (one course for several grade years, or the high school arts levels): one frame file for the
207:   band. The Units table gains a `Year` column right after `#` (`K`, `1`, `2`, or `Level 1` and so on), units are
208:   numbered through the band (unit 1 of year 2 follows the last unit of year 1), and each year is whole on its own:
209:   its own units, its own end-of-unit assessments and its own mid-year and year-end course-level checks. The
210:   **Days.** bullet gives the band's total first, then the days a year ("192: 64 a year for three years").
211:   `frame.py` spreads the band's days over that many school years and notes the unit days by year. A subject page
212:   that says "one frame per grade year" is met by this shape: each year's part of the file is that year's frame, and
213:   the scope and sequence later writes one year at a time.
214: - **Doses:** use the minutes, sessions a week and days a year the subject's STANDARD.md and APPROACH.md settle; never
215:   the core 160-day yardstick unless the subject sets it. A smaller course means fewer minutes a week, never fewer
216:   units (Cory 2026-11-15).
217: - **Course-level checks:** grades K to 8 after-core checks do not take the core subjects' target weeks; place them
218:   so they do not fall in a week (K to 5) where docs/courses/CALENDAR.md shows a core check for that grade, and say
219:   which weeks.
220: - **The seventh Units column** (lead 2026-11-25, from the pilot fix passes): arts heads it "Processes, anchors and
221:   works", physical education "Cues and settings", health "Skill steps", SEL "Core link and routines", computer
222:   science "Practices and concept areas", career education "Practices", ELD "Host unit and key language uses", library
223:   "Inquiry phases".
224: - **Owned standards cell:** a unit that owns no code (a carry-only or removable sensitive unit) says `None`; the
225:   check reads `None` and an empty cell alike.
226: - **A course-level check right after a unit** is that unit's end-of-unit assessment when it covers the unit, as in
227:   the core frames; say so in the unit's row and the check's "What it checks" cell. Units not followed by a check end
228:   in their own end-of-unit assessment.
229: - **Grades 6 to 12:** the year-end check may sit in week 32, the finals week (CALENDAR.md stagger rules 3 and 4).
230: - **After-core stagger:** each after-core course counts on its own in `frame.py calendar` (the five arts courses of a
231:   band are five checks, not one). Writers keep checks off core check weeks; the lead runs one calendar pass across
232:   the after-core frames for clashes among them.
233: - **Gates the subject sets:** a subject page that holds building until a condition is met (ELD: C11 proven in the
234:   core courses) still gets its frame now (Cory 2026-11-25: "please see if you can get them all completed"); the
235:   frame says in **For the lead** which later stage the condition holds.

