storyboard, shared with permission) and one improvement. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: emphasis and refining for clarity (grade 5). Tools: the revision log, the criteria card, the critique card and work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: drawn frames, pencil shading for contrast. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 6's capture skills and crew; the revision log and critique card return from year 3 one step harder (criteria on production steps). Before unit 8, whose public showing uses a piece arranged for an effect and the habit of judging steps | Candidate: a balance-and-contrast work from Asia or Africa, open access, for range | Iturbide's photograph shows a Zapotec woman of Juchitán: the sensitivity agent reads the artist's own words and a Juchitán or Zapotec source first; students study the photograph and do not imitate the dress. The work card for the Tetons names, in one line, the Shoshone and other peoples whose homelands the park is on, from the park's own page. Critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class. Hazards and controls: screen breaks; device cords taped down or none. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
289: | 8 | 4 | Show it to the school. *How do we join a school subject, an art form and media in one piece, and how do online spaces make meaning?* | 14 | ma-g4-present; ma-g4-sources | MA:Pr4.1.4; MA:Pr6.1.4a; MA:Pr6.1.4b; MA:Cn10.1.4a; MA:Cn10.1.4b | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10); creating practiced (review). Works: Skawennati, CyberPowWow (1997 to 2004; Skawennati is Kanien'kehá:ka (Mohawk) from Kahnawà:ke; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), an online gallery and meeting place built by Native artists, described and linked to the artist's own page; Jean Painlevé, L'Hippocampe (The Seahorse) (France, 1934), a science film of seahorses joined with music, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 3's blended piece and showing role (ma-g3-present, unit 4) and the source log (ma-g3-sources, unit 4). MA:Pr4.1.4: show how forms from a school subject, an art form and media can be coordinated in one piece (a science or social studies topic the student researched, a drawing, a tune or a movement, and a stop-motion clip, photo-story or sound piece). MA:Pr6.1.4a: explain the showing conditions for a public sharing in the school (a hallway screening or a family evening) and handle a role and its steps. MA:Pr6.1.4b: explain how the showing went and what to improve. MA:Cn10.1.4a: look into an interest, research it with two outside sources, and add cultural knowledge (the student's own, or a public or invented one, marked the same) to inform the piece, on the source log. MA:Cn10.1.4b: show how media makes meaning and cultural experience in online spaces (CyberPowWow as a space Native artists built for themselves; a Curio Class invented online space on printed screens). Words: coordinate, public, explain, research, cultural knowledge, online. Showing: the public school showing, with each family's yes. End-of-unit evidence (two check days; the showing is the first), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.4, a look-for marked at the showing: subject, art form and media coordinated; MA:Pr6.1.4a, the kept screening-plan card (conditions explained, the role's steps), scored with a look-for on the role carried out; MA:Pr6.1.4b, a talk item: how the showing went and one improvement; MA:Cn10.1.4a, the kept source log: two sources credited and one line on what each informed; MA:Cn10.1.4b, a written item: one way an online space makes meaning or a shared experience, with the example. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: two formats compared (grade 5). Tools: the screening-plan card, the source log, the invented online space screens and role cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: drawn frames, a box stage, a printed showing program. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in year 4 because the public showing uses the year's stop-motion and crew (unit 6) and an arranged piece (unit 7), and ends the year after the year-end check (week 25); the screening plan and source log return from year 3 one step harder (a public showing, research) | Candidate: the school subject topics drawn from what the grade's science and social studies courses teach that term, listed on a topic card | Skawennati: the sensitivity agent reads the artist's own pages and a Kahnawà:ke community source first; no student imitates Kanien'kehá:ka designs or dress; CyberPowWow is studied by description and the still images on the artist's page, and no student goes online in class. Cultural knowledge is optional, with a public or invented topic marked the same, and no student is asked to explain their family's culture. Family yes for the public showing and any recording. Hazards and controls: a clear floor if a movement is joined; cords taped down; sound at a comfortable level. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
328: 
329: ### Mathematics, grades 3 to 5
330: 
331: No unit asks for rates, fractions, scale or measuring. Frames and panels are counted, never divided into seconds;
332: the year 5 newscast's run sheet gives each segment's length as minutes read from the classroom clock, which grade 3
333: math teaches (telling time to the minute), so no unit uses math ahead of math (lesson 13).
334: 
335: ### Science and social studies, grades 3 to 5
336: 
337: Unit 8's coordinated piece draws its school-subject content from a topic the student chooses from what the grade's
338: science or social studies course has taught, on a topic card; nothing waits on a set unit, and the source cards
339: carry the facts used. Unit 12's broadside, winter count and newsreel are taught with their setting on work cards (bar
340: criterion 7), not leaned on social studies.
341: 
342: ### Computer science
343: 
344: STANDARD.md 6: media arts teaches the one idea of privacy or copyright its unit needs (the tool and space card, the
345: ethics card, the media-literacy checklist, the source log) in a short step of the unit and does not wait for
346: computer science, which is not core; the deeper unit is computer-science:g8-attribution.
347: 
348: ## For the lead
349: 
350: 1. **Media arts connecting ids swapped (settled), as in the K to 2 frame.** Checked at source (the California standards page for
351:    MA:Cn10 grade 3): anchor 10 is the student's own experience and anchor 11 society, culture and history, as in
352:    every other discipline. The words-file lines for MA:Cn10.1.* and MA:Cn11.1.* were swapped, every ma-*-sources
353:    row now carries the Cn10 ids and every ma-*-society row the Cn11 ids, and this frame's id labels changed to match
354:    (the pairs of rows stay in their units).
355: 2. **After-core check weeks.** Weeks chosen from the core calendar and the units' load: year 3 weeks 17 and 26, year
356:    4 weeks 17 and 25, year 5 weeks 18 and 25; the once-a-week respond sessions fall in weeks 18 and 27 (year 3), 18
357:    and 26 (year 4), 19 and 26 (year 5). Grade 3's year-end has only week 26 free of core checks with a free week
358:    after it (25 and 28 are core weeks). `frame.py calendar` shows after-core clashes in grade 3 weeks 17 and 26, grade 4 weeks 17 and 25 and grade 5
359:    week 18 (dance, theater, visual arts, physical education, social and emotional learning); left for the lead's one
360:    calendar pass.
361: 3. **Media arts weekly time figure.** STANDARD.md 3.4 has no media arts row; the visual and media arts association's
362:    elementary checklist (arteducators.org, "OTL-Elementary_Visual_Media_Arts_Program.pdf", the file the visual arts
363:    pilot read) is read at source for its media arts lines, then STANDARD.md gains a "| Media arts, 3 to 5 |" row and
364:    the Pending note in the Days bullet is replaced with the figure and both paths' gaps. A download, so not done here.
365: 4. **Paper path meets the tool, capture and online ids (item-level, decided, as the K to 2 frame).** A media tool
366:    here is anything that frames, captures, orders, plays or shows pictures and sound, so a device-free class meets
367:    MA:Pr5.1.3a to 5c, the Cn11 tool-and-space ids and the online ids in full on paper and printed invented screens.
368:    If the lead or Cory wants capture or editing met only on a device, the course needs one shared device per group
369:    of five, a cost question.
370: 5. **Work picks the unit plans must confirm on the item's page:** the summary of Obomsawin's Sigwan (unit 4), the
371:    film and page for Alassane's La Mort du Gandji (unit 2), which Ōfuji film (unit 6), and the living status of the
372:    Ghanaian poster painters (unit 9). Each is named as a pick with "the unit plan confirms"; a swap keeps the ids and
373:    the band's range.
374: 6. **Supplies ceiling (money, for Cory).** Provisional costs: $45 (year 3), $50 (year 4), $50 (year 5) a class of 30,
375:    all optional beside a free path. Pick: $50 a class a year for grades 3 to 5 media arts, like science K to 5's
376:    settled $50 ceiling, with every unit's free path kept.

