48:    `| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | C3 | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |`
49:    For ELA, replace C3 with Texts in this header; for science, head the C3 column Practices and crosscutting
50:    concepts.
51:    - **Skills:** the skill_id of every progression row the unit teaches, `; `-separated. Every row the course owns
52:      sits in exactly one unit. A unit that revisits an earlier row's skill names it in the text as review, never in
53:      this column.
54:    - **Owned standards:** the codes those rows own, as written in the rows.
55:    - **Why this unit, here:** why it is a unit and why it sits at this point (order of time, a skill or model it
56:      needs from an earlier unit, a seam).
57:    - **What students learn:** a lettered part of an owned standard (RL.4.1a, MA.WHII.T1.1a) is named by its code in
58:      the unit that teaches it, so the gate can see every part placed (standards in full).
59:    - Units in an order that uses a skill or model before it is taught fail the read.
60: 6. `## Course-level checks`: one table, columns exactly
61:    `| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |`, one row per check (mid-year, year-end), each
62:    with at least one respond day. A row with "review" in its Check cell holds review days (Check days counts them,
63:    Respond days is 0). In K to 8, place the checks on the target weeks of docs/courses/CALENDAR.md, so no week (K to 5)
64:    or day (6 to 8) holds two subjects' checks.
