16: ## What the arts carry (unit field 2)
17: 
18: - **The standards and their parts.** The unit lists each performance standard by id with its parts, one part per
19:   action, in one line of our own words (STANDARD.md section 2), and the limit (for example, "with a given pattern,
20:   not an invented one"). Every part has an end-of-unit item. The three things below sit under this.
21: 
22: An arts unit also carries three things, named in the unit plan:
23: 
24: - **The technique**: one skill of the discipline taught in steps that get harder (a brush stroke, a rhythm
25:   pattern, a vocal warm-up, a stage cross, a dance phrase, a camera shot).
26: - **The works studied**: two to four works by other artists (see "Works and their settings" and "Rights").
27: - **The showing**: what the class makes and shows or performs at the end, and to whom (the class, another class,
28:   families).
29: 
30: **Works and their settings.** Each year's works include living makers and more than one region and period; a unit
31: plan lists each work with its maker, place, date, purpose and rights line. A setting is when, where, by whom and
32: for what. No single tradition is the default. Where the open copy of a work is thin, the unit uses a description
33: and a link to the maker's or museum's own page, never a copy, and does not drop the work. Where a work has a sacred
34: or ceremonial use, the unit says what it is, studies it by looking and listening only, and does not have students
35: perform or imitate it. Students learn a tradition's form from the community's own published teaching or a named
36: practitioner's words, never by costume or invented imitation, and no student plays a character by an accent or a
37: stereotype. The unit plan records the works' makers and places in one line each. Every unit gets the fresh-agent
38: sensitivity read, which covers the works with the rest of the unit (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md).
39: Museum open-access images and public domain recordings lean to older, European and American works, so the year's
40: list is checked for range, not taken as the open sources give it (docs/reviews/arts-open-material-2026-11-16.md).
41: 
42: **Rights.** Every work a unit uses has a rights line in the unit plan, checked on the item's own page. A work that
43: is not public domain or openly licensed is described in our own words and linked to the maker's or museum's page,
44: never copied or embedded. Scripts, songs and dances students perform are public domain, openly licensed, original
45: to Curio Class or written by the students. A showing for families uses only those. Media arts remix uses only
46: public domain or openly licensed sources and names them. A student's work is the student's: it is shown outside the
47: class only with the family's yes, and carries the student's choice of name.
