156: ### Step 2. Course frames for the four core subjects, then a review: baseline 1
157: 
158: A course frame says what the course is: its grades, the standards and progression skills it owns, its days, its
159: unit list with a reason for each unit, and, where there is a base text, the source map (a section of the frame,
160: every base lesson placed in one of our units). The day-level data/plans/<course>/source-map.csv comes with the
161: scope and sequence, scripted from the source's free pages, then judged for unit edges and day counts; grade 4
162: math's table of 149 IM lessons is already scripted and kept outside the repo in the source store. A course frame is
163: the course-level summary; its scope and sequence (step 3) then sets every day in order. The 52 core courses of the
164: four subjects get one each (the 6 electives in these subjects wait, see After the core subjects). Scopes and
165: sequences already on file are read as input against the progressions, not assumed right.
166: 
167: Then **baseline 1**, a whole-structure review:
168: - every frame against its progression rows, and the frames of each subject read side by side through the grades;
169: - where the layers, the gates or the vocabulary need alignment or fixing;
170: - **the website design check**: whether the site's structure (catalog pages, topics, course and unit pages) still
171:   fits the hierarchy, and what changes if not. Proposals go to Cory; nothing is rebuilt during planning unless he
172:   says;
173: - a Codex read of a sample.
174: 
