FILE docs/FOUNDATION.md
1: # Curio Class: the foundation (layer 1)
2: 
3: Gathered 2026-11-11 (Cory). This is the one home for what Curio Class is for, who it serves, what it promises and
4: what it assumes. Every layer above it (the approach to teaching, the progressions, the scopes and sequences, the section plans, the
5: tools) is built on this page and may not contradict it. Until today these lines were spread over docs/VISION.md,
6: docs/ROADMAP.md, docs/ADOPTION-BAR.md and docs/agents/decisions/BASELINE.md; those files keep their detail and now
7: point here. Where one of them disagrees with this page, this page wins and the other file is fixed.
8: 
9: It changes rarely, and only with Cory. The layers and the order of work are in docs/ROADMAP.md ("The layers").
10: 
11: ## 1. What it is for
12: 
13: A free, complete, open K-12 classroom toolkit: interactive tools, printables, lessons and units for every standard,
14: so a teacher can run a full course without paying for curriculum or software (VISION, Mission).
15: 
16: **The end goal.** A teacher can run any course on Curio Class from the first day to the last, free, with no account,
17: and the course would stand up to a state adoption review. A course is a grade of a subject or a high school course.
18: Every core subject is planned top down (progression, course frames, scopes and sequences, unit plans) before days
19: are written (docs/PRODUCTION-PATH.md, Cory 2026-11-13); each course is finished as a whole before it is called
20: finished. The earlier pilot-first bend (2026-11-11) is retired; the probe and guard replace it.
21: 
22: **We own the full course** (Cory, 2026-11-11; base texts added 2026-11-12). Curio Class owns every course: its
23: shape, tone, format, gates and reads. Where a well-reviewed open program carries a licence as open as ours (CC BY
24: or public domain), it can be the course's **base text**: adapted into Curio Class days, molded to our needs and
25: voice, with a credit line that says it was changed (math: Illustrative Mathematics). Programs under non-commercial
26: or share-alike terms are evidence only, for order, time, models and anchors, never their wording or problems,
27: so every page stays CC BY 4.0. We do not stitch two programs together: each hangs together through its own routines and pictures, and a
28: mix is weaker than either. A tool built to a clear job in our unit will also fit the same lesson in a course a
29: teacher already uses, and that is welcome, but it is not the plan.
30: 
31: ## 2. Who it is for
32: 
33: | Who | What that means for the work |
34: |---|---|
35: | **A classroom teacher** running a course | The main reader of every scope and sequence, section plan and teacher page. Teacher pages teach the teacher |
36: | **A parent teaching one child at home** (named 2026-11-11; how far, decided 2026-11-12) | The second audience, served by the same course, never a separate track. Every lesson day carries an "at home" block (a 30 to 40 minute one-child path, what the parent says where the class has partner talk, which items to do, home swaps for materials); a course gives placement from its readiness checks, a numbered calendar ("36-week" in Cory's 2026-11-12 call; the school calendar is 160 days over 32 weeks, docs/courses/CALENDAR.md) and a record that works as a portfolio; answer keys explain, not only list; sample pieces show what grade-level work looks like. Not planned: separate home courses or multi-age plans |
37: | **A student alone** | The site is public and a child may open any tool with no adult present. Every tool stands alone: defaults work for a solo user and teacher toggles are extras (BASELINE rule 4) |
38: 
39: ## 3. The promises
40: 
41: These hold even where a review, a district system or a convenience would reward breaking them.
42: 
43: 1. **Free for good.** No paywall, ever (ROADMAP, Goal).
44: 2. **No accounts, no ads, no tracking, no student analytics** (VISION, "What we will not do"). A tool cannot
45:    remember a student from one day to the next and there is no live teacher dashboard. We chose that.
46: 3. **Results live with the student and the teacher.** A score code the student shows, and printable record sheets.
47:    The score code records only what a tool can mark (ROADMAP, "What a finished course holds", item 4).
48: 4. **Every tool works offline as a single file.** Where a tool stores anything, it says on screen that it never
49:    leaves the device (BASELINE rule 7).
50: 5. **Paper is a first-class product.** Worksheets, units and printables matter as much as screens. Printables read in
51:    black and white.
52: 6. **Honest words.** We say taught, practiced or shown, per the ledger, never more. Practice tools are not
53:    diagnoses and score codes are not proof. We never say Curio Class was reviewed by, meets or is endorsed by
54:    anyone. Public copy names no companies (BASELINE rule 15).
55: 7. **Classroom-safe, and plain about hard facts.** History and science are not softened; they are told with care
56:    for the people involved, and a fresh agent checks sensitive content before it goes live (BASELINE rules 5, 6, 8).
57: 8. **We do not copy a branded product** in name, look or text, and we respect every licence: read and link, adapt
58:    only where the licence allows.
59: 
60: ## 4. The bar
61: 
62: State adoption is the bar we build to (Cory, 2026-11-10; BASELINE rule 20). A full course should meet the substance
63: of what state and national curriculum reviews ask for. We do not pursue formal adoption now. The bar applies to
64: courses and the shelves that feed them; Curiosities and Recess are outside it. The bar never outranks the promises
65: above. What it asks and what we took from it: docs/ADOPTION-BAR.md. The rubrics we read ourselves against are filed
66: as docs/RUBRIC-*.md.
67: 
68: **Quality over speed, and over saved work** (BASELINE rules 1, 2, 14). Each piece is really good before the next
69: wave grows. An existing spec or tool carries no weight against what a unit needs: if the unit needs something
70: different, the old work loses.
71: 
72: ## 5. What we assume about the room
73: 
74: Decided 2026-11-11 (Cory) unless marked.
75: 
76: 1. **Grades 3 to 12: one device per student on a tool day.** The courses are designed that way and say so plainly.
77:    Every tool day still carries one line for a single projector, because some rooms have nothing else and a reviewer
78:    will ask.
79: 2. **Kindergarten to grade 2: one shared classroom device**, always with a paper path (Cory, 2026-11-14).
80: 3. **The physical world is written into the lesson plan.** A tool does not replace rolling the marble, using a
81:    protractor or reading a whole book. Hands-on work, materials and their cost are part of the unit, with a path for
82:    a teacher who has nothing (docs/reviews/science-4-spine-2026-11-11.md, finding 7).
83: 4. **A teacher judges open work.** A tool marks what has a right answer. For writing, argument and discussion, the
84:    tool gives the work a structure so it is quick to read, and print carries the rest: a rubric, sample pieces at
85:    each score level, and a look-for sheet. Our job is to make that judging fast and consistent (BASELINE rule 21
86:    already says a tool cannot mark writing or talk).
87: 5. **Time.** A scope and sequence states its own year and period length and gives a shorter path where ordinary schools have less
88:    time than a full program assumes.
89: 
90: ## 6. What a tool is for
91: 
92: Decided 2026-11-11 (Cory), from the discussion recorded in docs/agents/decisions/DESK-ANSWERS.md.
93: 
94: 1. **A tool is the end piece of instruction, a means to a defined end.** The end is defined in a section plan: what
95:    each day is for, where a tool is used and for what job. **No tool is built or refined until a section plan names
96:    its job.**
97: 2. **A tool can be whatever the unit needs.** The section plan asks, for each key moment, what the ideal instrument for
98:    that idea would be. Sometimes that is a tool nobody has specced; sometimes it is a marble and a ramp. The limits
99:    are the promises and assumptions on this page and the cost of doing a tool well, not what software can do.
100: 3. **Key tools and light tools.** A key tool is the one a day is planned around: it does the day's main job in a
101:    way print cannot (feedback on each try, help that fades as the student succeeds, many cases fast), and the paper
102:    path is the fallback (reworded 2026-11-11; it had read "one the unit cannot run without", which no tool can meet
103:    while every plan must also run on paper). Key tools are built first and hold the course up. Light tools (called
104:    practice tools before 2026-11-11) are lighter, built from shared templates over the long term, and still correct
105:    and still meet the three hooks. Lighter never means lower quality. A course is runnable when its key tools are
106:    live and print covers the rest. "Key" is a mark given by the section plan; the roles of BASELINE rule 21 (teach,
107:    see why, practice) still say what a tool does.
108: 4. **Curiosities and Recess** are play and wonder. They claim no standard and are outside the bar (VISION).
109: 5. **Tools and specs made before this page are sorted by demand** (Cory, 2026-11-11; BASELINE rule 26). Named by a
110:    section plan: course work. Live, passing the teaching test, not named: stays on the shelf, free-standing, and
111:    claims no course coverage. Everything else: frozen. A script makes the list; nobody argues a tool into a course.
112: 
113: ## 7. How the work is checked
114: 
115: 1. **The curriculum expert reads cold, often** (docs/agents/EXPERT.md). Cory's attention and a teacher's are the
116:    scarce things, so frequent cold reads by the expert, which teaches itself from outside sources first, are the
117:    main review at every layer. It is the main read, not the only one (changed 2026-11-11, when Cory accepted the cold strategic review). The
118:    writer and the expert are the same kind of model, so a cold read removes anchoring but not a shared mistake.
119:    Three things stand beside it: the sources the expert must open before it judges (the pedagogy and standards
120:    data, which Cory expects to grow), a script for everything that can be computed (item 2), a Codex read of a
121:    different model kind, and the agent reads in item 3.
122: 2. **A machine checks what a machine can** (BASELINE rule 27). Standard codes, day counts, coverage of every part
123:    of a standard, and every answer that can be computed. A key the script cannot reproduce fails the build.
124: 3. **Reads beside the expert** (Cory, 2026-11-11; BASELINE rule 28; changed 2026-11-14). Every row below is
125:    required before the course it touches is called finished. On 2026-11-14 Cory said there is no outside support
126:    ("We have no support. Our agents are our best hope for success"): the content rows are agent reads, each by a
127:    fresh agent that studies its sources first (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md). No outside human reader is
128:    planned. Pilots are retired (2026-11-13): the probe and guard (docs/pipeline/PROBE-GUARD.md) take their place.
129: 
130:    | Who | What they read or do | When |
131:    |---|---|---|
132:    | The expert agent (was a working teacher on the pilot unit) | Reads every plan and the probe and guard days against the subject lens and rubric | Every unit plan; the probe and the guard |
133:    | The answer-key agent (was a math content reviewer) | Solves every item of one whole course's checks cold and compares with the key, beside the script check | Before a math course is called finished |
134:    | The safety agent (was a science teacher) | Safety and materials of every investigation, against published school science safety guidance; and every PE unit with striking tools, mats, climbing, resistance training or an outdoor trip, against published school PE safety guidance (Cory, 2026-11-21) | Before a science course is called finished; before such a PE unit goes live |
135:    | The rights agent (was a second reader on rights) | The licence and source of every text a course teaches from | Before an ELA text is taught |
136:    | The Spanish agent (was a native Spanish reader) | Family notes, and student materials if they are made | Before Spanish is published in a course |
137:    | The sensitivity agent (was a health specialist) | The sensitive health topics, against public health guidance | Before those units go live |
138:    | The sensitivity agent (was a reader from the community concerned) | Native, cultural, disability and hard-history content, after studying the community's own published voice | Before those units go live |
139: 
140:    Rights and Spanish became agent reads on 2026-11-14 (DESK-ANSWERS, the continuity review's four questions); the
141:    funding rows for a paid Spanish review and teacher pilots stay, marked "only if funded".
142: 4. **Lower layers are solid enough to build on, and expected to be revised** by what is built on them. No layer is
143:    declared finished in the abstract.
144: 5. **What goes to Cory:** scope, audience, money, accounts, outside people, a live deploy, anything that moves a
145:    standard between sections, and anything this page cannot settle. Item-level choices inside the standards' limits
146:    are settled by the expert and reach Cory in a weekly digest (BASELINE, "What goes to Cory").
147: 
148: ## Open questions on this page
149: 
150: - Closed 2026-11-14: the device assumption for kindergarten to grade 2 (section 5).

