1: # Course frame: Media Arts, grades K to 2
2: 
3: Course `media-arts-grades-k-to-2` (catalogue id `media-arts-k-2`), a band course for kindergarten, grade 1 and grade 2, run as three years one after another (year K, year 1, year 2). Draft frame, written 2026-11-25; the scope and sequence sets the days later, one year at a time, and each year's part of this file is that year's frame (data/courses.csv, "one frame per grade year").
4: 
5: ## The course in brief
6: 
7: - **Units.** 12: four in each year. Every year has the same four-unit shape, so a technique and a thread come back
8:   one step harder a year later in the same place: a looking-and-tools unit, an ideas-and-production unit, a
9:   making-and-judging unit, and a screening unit that ends the year with the class showing. Each unit joins two
10:   progression threads, and every unit makes, studies another maker's media work and shows (bar criteria 4 and 5).
11:   Media arts here means pictures, sound and moving pictures framed, put in order, captured and shown; in K to 2 most
12:   of it is made with paper media tools (a card viewfinder, shadow puppets, picture cards in a paper stage, flip
13:   books, a paper film strip, a picture scroll), and a shared school device is one way in, never the only one.
14: - **Days.** 192: 64 a year for three years. Each year: 60 days in its four units, 2 course-level check days (one
15:   mid-year, one year-end) and 2 respond days, one after each check. One session of 40 minutes, two sessions a week,
16:   32 weeks a year (STANDARD.md 3.4: a K to 8 arts year is 64 course days; it never needs a daily period, bar
17:   criterion 9). The 40 minutes hold the set-up routine (about 5 minutes, devices and trays out), the teaching and
18:   making, and clean-up, hand-back of devices and collecting work (about 5 minutes in a paper or device session, 7 in
19:   a cutting and gluing session); a readiness or retrieval task takes ten minutes. Each unit's days include its
20:   end-of-unit assessment (and, in units over 25 days, its mid-unit assessments; no unit here is over 25 days) and one
21:   respond day after it. Unit days follow the rows' load: the second unit of each year is longest (22 days) because it
22:   owns five ids, two techniques and the production roles; the first (18) sets up the tool rules and the looking and
23:   listening routine; units 3 and 4 of each year (10 each) make from the plans and tool skills already built, and the
24:   screening unit's making is joining work. These are course days: the school calendar (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md)
25:   spreads them two a week, so course day n of a year falls in that year's week n / 2, rounded up. National time
26:   figure (STANDARD.md 3.4): Pending(docs/courses/arts/STANDARD.md#| Media arts, K to 2 |): the media arts weekly
27:   figure is read at source and stated here with its gap on both paths (80 minutes a week, 40 on the
28:   once-a-week path). The course is written so a classroom teacher can teach it; a school with a media arts teacher
29:   uses that teacher.
30: - **The once-a-week path.** A school that meets once a week keeps every unit and every id in 32 sessions a year by
31:   merging the days each unit marks (STANDARD.md 3.4). Each unit keeps, in this order and no fewer: the
32:   demonstration with a step card, opened by the look or listen at a work that carries the unit's response and its
33:   connecting sentence; one making day with the critique; the showing (the check); one respond day. Fluency days fold
34:   into the openers, and the two rows' understanding days merge into one. Sessions on that path, every year: units of
35:   9, 11, 4 and 4 sessions; each year's two checks keep 1 check session and 1 respond session. The check sessions fall
36:   in the same weeks as on the full path (weeks 21 and 27); the respond sessions fall one week later (weeks 22 and
37:   28). In the screening units (4, 8, 12), whose full path has two check days, the screening is the check session and
38:   the two talk items are heard at the teacher's table in the respond session's first 20 minutes while the others
39:   start their respond work from the screening marks. A merged day never drops a critique, a revision or a respond
40:   day; the scope and sequence marks which days merge.
41: - **Standard.** National Core Arts Standards, 2014, media arts performance standards, by id only, each named with
42:   its line in data/standards/ncas-performance-standards-words.txt, never quoted (STANDARD.md 2). The band-year rule
43:   binds (STANDARD.md 3.1): year K claims only the 18 kindergarten ids (`.K`, `.Ka` and so on), year 1 only the 18
44:   grade 1 ids, year 2 only the 18 grade 2 ids: 54 in all, the count in STANDARD.md 3.2, every one taught and
45:   assessed in its own year. A year revisits the year before's ids as practice that returns, never as a claim. No
46:   pre-kindergarten id is claimed. Every year touches all four artistic processes (creating, producing, responding,
47:   connecting; bar criterion 1), and each unit touches at least two. In the Units table the C3 column is headed
48:   "Processes, anchors and works": the artistic processes and anchor standards the unit works on, and the works it
49:   studies. Check set: each unit plan checks itself against the words-file lines of its ids, and each id's action
50:   against the national standard's own text where a words line may have drifted (For the lead item 1), the nine
51:   criteria of the arts part of docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md and the lens's error list (docs/agents/lenses/arts.md);
52:   the "Check set adds" column names what that is expected to add. Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds.
53:   scripts/standards.py checks every id against the id file (L83, done 2026-11-21); each year's scope and sequence
54:   still states its id count (18) against STANDARD.md 3.2.
55: - **Mixed-grade classes.** A school that teaches the band as one mixed class runs the three years in turn (STANDARD.md
56:   3.1); each year still claims only its grade's ids, so a child's record shows which grade's standards they met. The
57:   scope and sequence says this once, on its first page.
58: - **Base text.** None. Media arts is Curio Class's own (docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md; the open-material scout,
59:   docs/reviews/arts-open-material-2026-11-16.md, found material to study, not plans to follow). Works studied are
60:   public domain films, photographs and posters from library and archive collections (the Library of Congress, film
61:   archives), and, for living or recent makers, a description in our own words with a link to the maker's, museum's
62:   or film board's own page, never a copy or an embed (APPROACH "Rights"). Each work named below is a pick the unit
63:   plan keeps unless its rights line, checked on the item's own page, fails; a swapped work must keep the unit's ids
64:   met and the year's range. Each year's works include living makers and more than one region and period (APPROACH
65:   "Works and their settings"); across the band they come from Africa (Mali, Nigeria), the Americas (Kiowa and
66:   Apsáalooke makers, Mexico, Canada, the United States), Asia (Japan, India, Korea) and Europe, since open film and
67:   photograph collections lean to old European and American work. Media remix uses only public domain or openly
68:   licensed sources, named on the piece. Curio Class writes every step card, work card, picture card set, record sheet
69:   and family note.
70: - **Techniques that get harder (bar criterion 2).** Framing: holding a card viewfinder and choosing what is inside
71:   the frame (K), far, middle and close shots drawn or taken (1), placing the main part big or small, at the center or
72:   the edge, to draw the eye (2). Order and motion: shadow puppets on a stick and a three-box storyboard (K), a flip
73:   book of four to eight pages and a six-frame strip with a repeat (1), paper-cutout stop-motion (move a little,
74:   capture, move again) from a storyboard (2). Sound: a listening walk with a picture sound map (K), a found or voice
75:   sound made on cue at a page turn (1), a sound recorded and shaped (louder, softer, a longer pause) or written as a
76:   sound score and performed the same way twice (2). Presenting: a pull-through paper film strip screened with named
77:   roles (K), a picture-book tale shown with one task and the showing conditions (1), a narrated picture-scroll
78:   screening with conditions named and sources credited (2).
79: - **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md ("teaching day") and APPROACH "Kinds of day". Understanding: a technique shown
80:   in named steps with the step card, or a work studied with the unit's words (the artistic process "responding" is
81:   taught here, never on a respond day). Fluency: short repeated drills of a technique with feedback (a "far, middle,
82:   close" framing call with the viewfinder, a three-card order drill, a flip-book page drill, a name-that-sound
83:   listening game); in K to 2 it is mostly the five-minute opener and one or two fluency days a unit. Application:
84:   making with some choice; the second making day holds the unit's one critique (describe, ask, suggest), and the
85:   next day's revision is application too. Check: the self-check and the talk and table items of the unit's evidence
86:   line; a kept piece is scored after the session, and a screening is scored where it happens. Respond: a second try
87:   at the part marked "not yet". A unit runs roughly: understanding, fluency, application, fluency, application with
88:   the critique, revision, check, respond.
89: - **Scored evidence.** Every owned id has an end-of-unit item or a look-for on the unit's guide, named id by id in
90:   the unit's "End-of-unit evidence" line. A progression row's "Assessed by" note names one form for the row, not one
91:   item for each id, so it is never the whole line. Items come in four kinds: a look-for on the guide (three to five
92:   look-fors, met, partly or not yet), scored from the kept piece after the session; a kept card or sheet made on the
93:   unit's last making days (a storyboard, a screening-plan card, a source card), scored with the piece; talk or table
94:   items on an unseen public domain, openly licensed or Curio Class original piece, at most two talk items a child
95:   on a check day; and, in units 2, 4, 8 and 12, look-fors marked at a screening. A talk-and-table check day's 40
96:   minutes: set-up 5; each child's self-check on the guide 5; six groups of five at the teacher's table for about four
97:   minutes each (24), where each child answers the talk items and, in units 1, 5 and 9, does the safe-use table item
98:   with one tool from a tray; clean-up 4. That is 38 minutes. The others finish or label work with paper and crayon,
99:   so a talk-and-table check day has no device out but the one on the teacher's table, and the teacher watches one
100:   table, never a room and a table at once. A screening check day (units 2, 4, 8 and 12, the first of their two check
101:   days): set-up 5; six groups of five screen in turn at the one screen, stage or box for about four minutes each
102:   (24) while the rest are the audience and the teacher marks each child's look-fors at the screening place; each
103:   child's self-check 5; clean-up 4. No talk items are heard that day; they come on the unit's second check day. The
104:   piece's draft, its spoken critique with the teacher's look-for marks on the record sheet, and its revision come on
105:   earlier days counted in the unit. No score comes from the day a technique is first taught. Each unit opens with a
106:   baseline look at its main look-for during the first practice days (APPROACH "Growth"), so every child has two marks
107:   a unit and the growth is the change. One piece a unit goes into the portfolio folder (paper, or a file on the
108:   school's device) with a one-line reflection, spoken to the teacher or a partner in K to 2 (bar criterion 8).
109: - **Readiness and retrieval.** Year K assumes no earlier media arts learning (pre-kindergarten ids are not taught; a
110:   kindergarten unit may use one as a readiness reference). Every year 1 and year 2 unit assumes the year before's
111:   rows in the same threads, last used one grade back, so it opens with a retrieval task inside day 2's opener, never
112:   a readiness check, and day 1 stays clean for the hook and the question. The count starts from the band's order
113:   (K, then 1, then 2). In a mixed class running the years in turn, a child may meet year 2 before year 1: each
114:   retrieval task then carries its ten-minute reteach of the earlier technique as written (APPROACH "Readiness and the
115:   baseline look"). The Seams table gives each row's last use.
116: - **Course-level checks.** Grades K to 8 after-core checks avoid the weeks where docs/courses/CALENDAR.md shows a
117:   core check for the grade (K: 14 to 17 and 29 to 32; grade 1: 14 to 16, 19 and 29 to 32; grade 2: 14 to 16, 18 to
118:   20, 28, 29, 31 and 32). Mid-year: week 21 in every year (course days 41 and 42 of the year). Year-end: week 27 in
119:   every year (days 53 and 54). Weeks 29 to 32 hold every core subject's year-end check in K to 2, so the year-end
120:   check comes before the screening unit (CALENDAR.md stagger tool c), and the screening unit's showing, in weeks 28
121:   to 32, is its own end-of-unit assessment.
122: - **Recording and kept work.** No child is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or a
123:   voice is never required (hands, the work, a shadow or the sound alone will do); a child who does not want to be
124:   recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands. Media in the room points at things, places and work,
125:   never at a child who has not said yes. Kept work stays on paper or on the school's or family's device and goes
126:   nowhere else (APPROACH "Kept work"); no child posts or shares media of others online (the rows' teacher notes).
127: - **Leave-out.** A school or family may leave out a unit or a work for its community's reasons (STANDARD.md 5); each
128:   unit stands alone. Units that a course page names: unit 4 (the screening joins a class movement piece to the film
129:   strip; a found-sound track in place of the movement keeps every id met), unit 9 (the artist's photographs show
130:   Apsáalooke dress that may carry ceremonial meaning, studied by looking only) and unit 10 (ideas are acted out to
131:   improvise them; quick sketches keep every id met without acting). Swapping a work keeps the unit's ids met; leaving
132:   a unit out means the course page and the unit's family note name the ids not claimed that year.
133: - **The ordinary room.** A class of 30 at tables, paper, pencils, crayons, card from cereal boxes, found objects for
134:   sound; nothing must be bought, and every unit names a free stand-in (STANDARD.md 3.4, APPROACH "Supplies"). One
135:   child at home: the parent is the audience and the critique partner, every partner or team task has a doubled
136:   version (parent and child), and the showing is to the family. The teacher needs no media arts training: each
137:   unit's teacher page says what to look and listen for, in plain words.
138: - **Supplies and cost.** Provisional cost per unit for a class of 30 (consumables, reusable items such as six
139:   flashlights counted once, printing of step cards, work cards and picture cards), all optional beside the free
140:   path: year K $5, $15, $5, $10 ($35); year 1 $5, $10, $5, $10 ($30); year 2 $10, $10, $5, $15 ($40). Planning
141:   figures, not quotes; any ceiling is under For the lead.
142: - **Devices.** None per student. Where a school has them, one shared school device (a camera, a sound recorder or a
143:   free drawing program) per group of five is one way in; one teacher screen may show a work, a step or a playback.
144:   Every day runs from the printed step card, work card and paper media tools without them, and no student account is
145:   used. The owned codes that name capture, recording or a tool (MA:Cr3.1.Ka, MA:Cr3.1.1a, MA:Pr5.1.Ka to Kc,
146:   MA:Pr5.1.1a to 1c, MA:Pr5.1.2a to 2c) are met in full on paper: in this course a media tool is anything that
147:   frames, captures, orders, plays or shows pictures and sound, so a paper-path child captures by drawing what the
148:   viewfinder frames or by a shadow held on the screen, records a sound as a picture-mark sound score performed the
149:   same way twice, and names and shows each method as a device user does (For the lead item 5). Shared devices are
150:   school-approved; each is wiped and handed back by the device card's steps.
151: - **Paper path.** Every day runs on paper, card, objects and printed cards; any Curio Class tool a unit plan adds (a
152:   frame-sequence viewer that plays drawn frames in order, a work viewer) has a printed version that does the same job
153:   (the flip book, the paper film strip) and needs no student account.
154: - **Reading load.** Step cards are picture-led with the words read aloud for K to 2; no child reads a card alone.
155:   English learners: each unit teaches its six words in depth, with a picture, a gesture or a sound and a frame ("I
156:   see ___ because ___." "It sounds ___."), a child may show the word instead of saying it, and the check marks what
157:   the child made apart from what they said (APPROACH "Supports").
158: - **Safety read.** Every unit is hands-on, so every unit gets the fresh safety agent's read
159:   (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md) against three free guides, named here and opened by the agent: the
160:   federal consumer product safety agency's "Art and Craft Safety Guide" (cpsc.gov), the World Health Organization's
161:   "Make Listening Safe" pages on safe headphone volume (who.int), and the web accessibility guidelines' rule on
162:   flashing, success criterion 2.3.1 "Three Flashes or Below Threshold" (w3.org). Rules for every unit, from APPROACH
163:   "Safe use" ("from memory" until that read checks them): no filming, photographing or recording a child without
164:   the family's yes; no flashing light in any piece, screen or shadow play, and no strobe; screen breaks, with no
165:   child at a screen for more than about ten minutes at a stretch; headphones, where used, at low volume, and no loud
166:   sound in a child's ear; flashlights are low-power and never shone in eyes, and no laser pointers; device cords
167:   taped down or none; card cut by an adult with a blade, children use blunt-tip scissors; supplies labeled
168:   non-toxic; hands washed after glue and found objects; no small items in the mouth. A movement day (unit 4) uses a
169:   clear floor with room to move arms out, no jumping from heights and no partner contact. Each unit's last column
170:   gives its hazards and controls in the words days.csv will copy. No student sign-off form.
171: - **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md),
172:   which covers the works with the rest of the unit; no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Units with a
173:   cultural or community tradition name the communities and the sources the agent studies first: unit 1 (Bamako
174:   studio portraiture, Mali), unit 2 (the Arabian Nights setting of a 1926 German film), unit 3 (kamishibai, Japan),
175:   unit 5 (Kiowa photographs, Oklahoma), unit 7 (Nigerian hairstyles photographed), unit 8 (a Mexican maker's book on
176:   Frida Kahlo), unit 9 (Apsáalooke photographs; 1930s United States posters), unit 10 (an Indian animated film) and
177:   unit 11 (a Korean-born American video artist). Every task that ties media to a child's own life or day (units 1, 4,
178:   5, 8, 9 and 12) offers an invented, story-character or class-shared option marked the same (APPROACH "Sensitive
179:   ground"). No task asks what a family watches, owns or posts, and home media is never judged. Each section carries
180:   health's steps for "If a student tells you something" (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md) on its teacher page.
181: - **Logic model (one page in the scope and sequence).** Inputs: two 40-minute sessions a week, step cards, work
182:   cards, paper media tools, found objects, an optional shared device. Activities: technique taught in steps, works
183:   studied with their setting, making with a critique and a revision, a showing or screening each unit. Outputs: a
184:   portfolio folder of 12 pieces over the band with reflections, 12 showings, two marks a unit per child on the main
185:   look-for. Outcome claimed: met marks on the year's 18 ids and growth on the look-fors. Nothing here has been tried
186:   with children, and Curio Class makes no claim that arts study raises any other result (APPROACH "Sources").
