335:    30, all optional beside a free path. Pick: the same ceiling as the visual arts pilot's pick, $75 a class a year,
336:    with every unit's free path kept.

FILE docs/courses/media-arts-grades-6-to-8/frame.md
1: # Course frame: Media Arts, grades 6 to 8
2: 
3: Course `media-arts-grades-6-to-8` (catalogue id `media-arts-6-8`), a band course for grades 6, 7 and 8, run as three years one after another (year 6, year 7, year 8). Draft frame, written 2026-11-25; the scope and sequence sets the days later, one year at a time, and each year's part of this file is that year's frame (data/courses.csv, "one frame per grade year").
4: 
5: ## The course in brief
6: 
7: - **Units.** 12: four in each year. Every year has the same four-unit shape, so a technique and a thread come back
8:   one step harder a year later in the same place: a reading-and-society unit, an ideas-and-production unit, a
9:   making-and-judging unit, and a screening unit that ends the year with the class showing. Each unit joins two
10:   progression threads, as the K to 2 media arts frame does, and every unit makes, studies another maker's media
11:   work and shows (bar criteria 4 and 5). Media arts here means pictures, sound and moving pictures framed, put in
12:   order, captured, edited and shown: photo sequences, stop-motion and drawn animation, audio pieces, short videos,
13:   posters and screen layouts. In grades 6 to 8 a shared school device is the usual way in where a school has one,
14:   and every unit has a full paper path (storyboards, flip books and drawn animatics, photo-essay layouts, sound
15:   scores performed live, a paper screen), so no id waits on a device.
16: - **Days.** 192: 64 a year for three years. Each year: 60 days in its four units, 2 course-level check days (one
17:   mid-year, one year-end) and 2 respond days, one after each check. One session of 40 minutes, two sessions a week,
18:   32 weeks a year (STANDARD.md 3.4: a K to 8 arts year is 64 course days; it never needs a daily period, bar
19:   criterion 9). The 40 minutes hold the set-up routine (about 5 minutes: devices, trays and the day's step card
20:   out), the teaching and making, and clean-up, hand-back of devices and collecting work (about 5 minutes, 7 in a
21:   cutting and gluing or stop-motion session); a retrieval task takes ten minutes inside an opener. Each unit's days
22:   include its end-of-unit assessment (and, in units over 25 days, its mid-unit assessments; no unit here is over
23:   25 days) and one respond day after it. Unit days follow the rows' load: the ideas-and-production unit of each year
24:   is longest (18 days) because it owns five ids, the planning portfolio and the production roles, and its team piece
25:   needs plan, prototype, production and a critique before the check; the reading-and-society unit (14) holds a
26:   written analysis and a short made investigation with its draft, critique and revision; the making-and-judging unit
27:   (14) makes from the plans and tool skills already built; the screening unit (14) owns five ids, but its making is
28:   joining content already gathered, and its screening is one of its two check days. These are course days: the
29:   school calendar (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md) spreads them two a week, so course day n of a year falls on that year's
30:   school day 2.5 x n, rounded up. National time figure (STANDARD.md 3.4): Pending(docs/courses/arts/STANDARD.md#| Media arts, 6 to 8 |): the media arts weekly figure for middle school
31:   is read at source in the visual and media arts association's middle school checklist and stated here with its gap
32:   on both paths (80 minutes a week, 40 on the once-a-week path). The course is written so a teacher with no media
33:   arts training can teach it; a school with a media arts teacher uses that teacher.
34: - **The once-a-week path.** A school that meets once a week keeps every unit and every id in 32 sessions a year by
35:   merging the days each unit marks (STANDARD.md 3.4). Each unit keeps, in this order and no fewer: the
36:   demonstration with a step card, opened by the look or listen at a work that carries the unit's response and its
37:   connecting sentence; one making day with the critique; the showing (the check); one respond day. Fluency days fold
38:   into the openers, and the two rows' understanding days merge into one. Sessions on that path, every year: units of
39:   7, 9, 6 and 6 sessions; each year's two checks keep 1 check session and 1 respond session. The mid-year check
40:   session falls in week 17, as on the full path, and its respond session in week 18; the year-end check session
41:   falls in week 31 and its respond session in week 32 (week 31 holds no core check in grades 6 to 8). In the
42:   screening units (4, 8, 12), whose full path has two check days, the screening is the check session and the written
43:   items are done in the respond session's first 20 minutes while the teacher starts the respond work from the
44:   screening marks. A merged day never drops a critique, a revision or a respond day; the scope and sequence marks
45:   which days merge.
46: - **Standard.** National Core Arts Standards, 2014, media arts performance standards, by id only, each named with
47:   its line in data/standards/ncas-performance-standards-words.txt, never quoted (STANDARD.md 2). The band-year rule
48:   binds (STANDARD.md 3.1): year 6 claims only the 18 grade 6 ids, year 7 only the 18 grade 7 ids, year 8 only the 18
49:   grade 8 ids: 54 in all, the count in STANDARD.md 3.2, every one taught and assessed in its own year. A year
50:   revisits the year before's ids as practice that returns, never as a claim. Every year touches all four artistic
51:   processes (creating, producing, responding, connecting; bar criterion 1), and each unit touches at least two. In
52:   the Units table the C3 column is headed "Processes, anchors and works": the artistic processes and anchor
53:   standards the unit works on, and the works it studies. Check set: each unit plan checks itself against the
54:   words-file lines of its ids, and each id's action against the national standard's own text where a words line may
55:   have drifted (For the lead item 1), the nine criteria of the arts part of docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md and the
56:   lens's error list (docs/agents/lenses/arts.md); the "Check set adds" column names what that is expected to add.
57:   Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds. scripts/standards.py checks every id against the id file (L83, done
58:   2026-11-21); each year's scope and sequence still states its id count (18) against STANDARD.md 3.2.
59: - **Mixed-grade classes.** A school that teaches the band as one mixed class runs the three years in turn (STANDARD.md
60:   3.1); each year still claims only its grade's ids, so a student's record shows which grade's standards they met.
61:   The scope and sequence says this once, on its first page.
62: - **Base text.** None. Media arts is Curio Class's own (docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md; the open-material scout,
63:   docs/reviews/arts-open-material-2026-11-16.md, found material to study, not plans to follow). Works studied are
64:   public domain films and photographs from library, archive and agency collections (the Library of Congress, the
65:   federal space agency's image and video library), and, for living or recent makers, a description in our own words
66:   with a link to the maker's, museum's or film board's own page, never a copy or an embed (APPROACH "Rights"). A
67:   work is shown in class only as the holding page offers it (a public domain copy, or the film board's own free
68:   viewing page opened by the teacher), and a long film is met through excerpts the unit plan names. Each work named
69:   below is a pick the unit plan keeps unless its rights line, checked on the item's own page, fails; a swapped work
70:   must keep the unit's ids met and the year's range. Each year's works include living makers and more than one
71:   region and period (APPROACH "Works and their settings"); across the band they come from Africa (Senegal, Kenya),
72:   the Americas (Inuk, Ho-Chunk and Cree makers, Canada, the United States), Asia (India, Japan, China), Europe (France, Ireland, Spain, the United Kingdom, Ukraine),
73:   and none repeats a K to 2 or 3 to 5 media arts pick or maker. Open film and photograph collections lean to old
74:   European and American work, so the living and recent picks carry the range. Media remix uses only public domain
75:   or openly licensed sources, named on the piece. Curio Class writes every step card, work card, case card,
76:   invented feed, record sheet and family note.
77: - **Techniques that get harder (bar criterion 2).** Framing and point of view: shot sizes and angles named and used
78:   for a point of view (6), a shot list that carries a story's structure (7), framing and style conventions blended
79:   for unity (8). Order and motion: a storyboard and a mockup tested against a purpose, with a 12-drawing flip book
80:   or stop-motion sequence (6), a prototype (a drawn or photographed animatic) judged against resources (7), a whole
81:   workflow planned, prototyped and critiqued (8). Sound: a sound layer (voice, found sound, silence) placed to back a
82:   message (6), sound stressed for an audience and setting (7), sound shaped to a style and polished for level and
83:   timing (8). Presenting: one screening with a showing plan comparing formats and a written review (6), a format
84:   chosen by evaluation with a growth review (7), one theme shown in several formats with an improvement put in place
85:   (8).
86: - **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md ("teaching day") and APPROACH "Kinds of day". Understanding: a technique shown
87:   in named steps with the step card, or a work studied with the unit's words (the artistic process "responding" is
88:   taught here, never on a respond day). Fluency: short repeated drills of a technique with feedback (a shot-size and
89:   angle call, a thumbnail storyboard sprint, a cut-on-the-beat timing drill, a sound-level match, a credit-line
90:   drill); it is mostly the five-minute opener and one or two fluency days a unit. Application: making and producing
91:   with some choice; the second making day holds the unit's one critique (describe, ask, suggest), and the next
92:   day's revision is application too. Check: the self-check and the written and table items of the unit's evidence
93:   line; a kept piece is scored after the session, and a screening is scored where it happens. Respond: a second try
94:   at the part marked "not yet". A unit runs roughly: understanding, fluency, application, fluency, application with
95:   the critique, revision, check, respond.
96: - **Scored evidence.** Every owned id has an end-of-unit item or a look-for on the unit's guide, named id by id in
97:   the unit's "End-of-unit evidence" line. A progression row's "Assessed by" note names one form for the row, not one
98:   item for each id, so it is never the whole line. Items come in four kinds: a look-for on the guide (three to five
99:   look-fors, met, partly or not yet), scored from the kept piece after the session; a kept card or sheet made on the
100:   unit's last making days (a planning portfolio page, a role record, a showing plan, a source log, a media-ethics
101:   check), scored with the piece; written items on an unseen public domain, openly licensed or Curio Class original
102:   piece, at most two written items a student on a check day; and, in units 4, 8 and 12, look-fors marked at a
103:   screening. A written check day's 40 minutes: set-up 3; each student's self-check on the guide 5; the written items
104:   25, in silence, with the teacher reading the room; collecting and clean-up 5. That is 38 minutes, and nothing is
105:   scored live. A screening check day (units 4, 8 and 12, the first of their two check days): set-up 5; five teams of
106:   six screen in turn at the one screen, wall or paper stage for about five minutes each (25) while the rest are the
107:   audience and the teacher marks each student's look-fors at the screening place; collecting and devices back 5;
108:   each student's self-check is written that evening or in the next session's opener. No written items are given that
109:   day; they come on the unit's second check day. The piece's draft, its critique with the teacher's look-for marks
110:   and written feedback on the record sheet, and its revision come on earlier days counted in the unit. No score
111:   comes from the day a technique is first taught. Each unit opens with a baseline look at its main look-for during
112:   the first practice days (APPROACH "Growth"), so every student has two marks a unit and the growth is the change.
113:   One piece a unit goes into the portfolio (paper, or a file on the school's device) with a one-line written
114:   reflection (bar criterion 8).
115: - **Readiness and retrieval.** Every year 6 unit assumes the grade 5 rows of its threads (Media Arts, grades 3 to 5,
116:   year 5), last used one grade back, so it opens with a retrieval task inside day 2's opener, never a readiness
117:   check, and day 1 stays clean for the hook and the question. Media Arts, grades 3 to 5 is not a core course, so this
118:   is a "by course order" seam (lesson 32): each year 6 retrieval task carries its ten-minute reteach of the grade 5
119:   technique as written, for a student new to media arts (APPROACH "Readiness and the baseline look"). Every year 7
120:   and year 8 unit assumes the year before's rows in the same threads, one grade back, and opens the same way. The
121:   count starts from the band's order (6, then 7, then 8). In a mixed class running the years in turn, a student may
122:   meet year 8 before year 7: each retrieval task then carries its reteach. The Seams table gives each row's last use.
123: - **Course-level checks.** Grades 6 to 8 after-core checks stay off the school days where docs/courses/CALENDAR.md
124:   shows a core check for the grade (stagger rule 3, by school day), and week 32 is a finals week that may hold every
125:   subject's year-end check. Mid-year: course days 33 and 34 of each year, school days 83 and 85 (week 17); no core
126:   subject holds a check day on school day 83 in grade 6, 7 or 8 (the nearest are grade 6 math on 80 and grade 6
127:   science on 90). Year-end: course days 63 and 64 of each year, school days 158 and 160 (week 32, the finals week).
128:   Each year's screening unit ends in its own screening and written check before the year-end check, which then
129:   draws its items from the year's first three units.
130: - **Recording and kept work.** No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or a
131:   voice is never required (hands, the work, a shadow, an object or the sound alone will do); a student who does not
132:   want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands. Media in the room points at things, places,
133:   hands and work, never at a student who has not said yes, and never at another class or a passer-by. Filming is in
134:   the school's own spaces with the teacher's leave; any home filming is optional, with the family. Kept work stays
135:   on paper or on the school's or family's device and goes nowhere else (APPROACH "Kept work"); no student posts or
136:   shares media of others online, and no task asks a student to post anything (the rows' teacher notes).
137: - **Leave-out.** A school or family may leave out a unit or a work for its community's reasons (STANDARD.md 5); each
138:   unit stands alone. Units that a course page names: unit 4 (it studies child labor photographs and a memorial to
139:   children killed in a church bombing; the second work may be swapped for another public domain historical
140:   photograph set and keep every id met), unit 5 (it studies social platforms through an invented feed; a school
141:   that keeps social media out of class swaps the feed for an invented school notice board, and the ids stay met)
142:   and unit 12 (its photographs show India's independence, which came with the partition of the country; the
143:   photographs may be swapped for another public domain set of a local event seen worldwide and keep every id met). Swapping a work keeps the unit's ids met; leaving a unit out means the course
144:   page and the unit's family note name the ids not claimed that year.
145: - **The ordinary room.** A class of 30 at tables, paper, pencils, card from cereal boxes, found objects for sound,
146:   string and clips for a paper screen; nothing must be bought, and every unit names a free stand-in (STANDARD.md 3.4,
147:   APPROACH "Supplies"). One student at home: the parent is the audience and the critique partner, every team task
148:   has a solo or doubled version (parent and student; the student holds every production role in turn), and the
149:   showing is to the family. The teacher needs no media arts training: each unit's teacher page says what to look
150:   and listen for, in plain words.
151: - **Supplies and cost.** Provisional cost per unit for a class of 30 (consumables, reusable items such as six clip
152:   lamps or flashlights for stop-motion counted once, printing of step cards, work cards, case cards and storyboard
153:   sheets), all optional beside the free path: year 6 $10, $15, $5, $10 ($40); year 7 $10, $15, $5, $10 ($40); year 8
154:   $10, $15, $5, $15 ($45). Planning figures, not quotes; any ceiling is under For the lead.
155: - **Devices.** None per student. Where a school has them, one shared school device (a camera, a sound recorder, or a
156:   free editing or drawing program on a school computer) per team of five or six is one way in; one teacher screen
157:   may show a work, a step or a playback. Every day runs from the printed step card, work card and paper media tools
158:   without them, and no student account is used, for any Curio Class tool or any outside service. The owned codes
159:   that name tools, technical quality or tool spaces (MA:Pr5.1.6c, MA:Pr5.1.7c, MA:Pr5.1.8c, MA:Cr3.1.8b,
160:   MA:Cn11.1.6b, MA:Cn11.1.7b, MA:Cn11.1.8b) are met in full on paper: in this course a media tool is anything that
161:   frames, captures, orders, plays or shows pictures and sound, so a paper-path student uses the viewfinder, the flip
162:   book, the drawn animatic and the sound score in ordinary and experimental ways, polishes technical quality as
163:   framing, timing, legibility and sound level in a live or paper showing, and analyzes tool spaces, social platforms
164:   and virtual worlds through Curio Class case cards and an invented paper feed (For the lead item 4). Shared
165:   devices are school-approved; each is wiped and handed back by the device card's steps.
166: - **Paper path.** Every day runs on paper, card, objects and printed cards; any Curio Class tool a unit plan adds (a
167:   frame-sequence viewer that plays drawn frames in order, a shot-list sheet, a work viewer) has a printed version
168:   that does the same job (the flip book, the paper film strip, the printed shot list) and needs no student account.
169: - **Reading load.** Step cards are picture-led with short numbered steps; work cards and case cards are at about the
170:   grade's reading level, one page, read aloud on day 1 for any student who needs it. English learners: each unit
171:   teaches its six words in depth, with a picture, a gesture or a sound and a frame ("The shot is ___ so the viewer
172:   ___." "I changed ___ because ___."), a student may show the word instead of saying it, and the check marks what the
173:   student made apart from what they wrote (APPROACH "Supports").
174: - **Safety read.** Every unit is hands-on, so every unit gets the fresh safety agent's read
175:   (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md) against three free guides, named here and opened by the agent: the
176:   federal consumer product safety agency's "Art and Craft Safety Guide" (cpsc.gov), the World Health Organization's
177:   "Make Listening Safe" pages on safe headphone volume (who.int), and the web accessibility guidelines' rule on
178:   flashing, success criterion 2.3.1 "Three Flashes or Below Threshold" (w3.org). Rules for every unit, from APPROACH
179:   "Safe use" ("from memory" until that read checks them): no filming, photographing or recording a student without
180:   the family's yes; no flashing light in any piece, screen or shadow play, and no strobe, and any work shown is
181:   checked against the flash rule first; screen breaks, with no student at a screen for more than about fifteen
182:   minutes at a stretch; headphones, where used, at low volume, and no loud sound in a student's ear; lamps and
183:   flashlights are low-power, never shone in eyes and kept off paper, and no laser pointers; device and lamp cords
184:   taped down or none; card cut by an adult or by a student with safety scissors, blades only by an adult; supplies
185:   labeled non-toxic; hands washed after glue and found objects. Each unit's last column gives its hazards and
186:   controls in the words days.csv will copy. No student sign-off form.
187: - **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md),
188:   which covers the works with the rest of the unit; no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Units with a
189:   cultural, community or hard-history setting name the communities and the sources the agent studies first: unit 1
190:   (Inuit of Igloolik, Nunavut, with an Inuk filmmaker; a Hindu epic story in an Indian film), unit 3
191:   (a Soviet-era film made in Ukraine), unit 4 (child workers in the United States, 1908 to 1916; the 1963 bombing of
192:   the 16th Street Baptist Church, Birmingham, Alabama), unit 5 (Dakar, Senegal, a city cart driver; social
193:   platforms), unit 6 (a Ho-Chunk filmmaker and the Ho-Chunk language), unit 7 (India, two boys of different means),
194:   unit 9 (a Black government worker in segregated Washington, 1942; a Cree filmmaker on how films show Native
195:   people), unit 10 (Kenya, a future of water scarcity), unit 11 (a Chinese classic novel and Peking opera; Irish
196:   folklore) and unit 12 (India's independence in 1947 and the partition that came with it; the 1969 Moon landing broadcast). Every task that ties
197:   media to a student's own life, identity or community (units 1, 4, 8, 9 and 12) offers an invented, public or
198:   class-shared option marked the same (APPROACH "Sensitive ground"). No task asks what a family watches, owns or
199:   posts, which platforms a student uses, or anything about a student's own online life, and home media is never
200:   judged. Civic and environmental units (9) study how media serves values; no student is asked to take or argue a
201:   side on a current political question, and the values rule holds. Each section carries health's steps for "If a
202:   student tells you something" (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md) on its teacher page.
203: - **Logic model (one page in the scope and sequence).** Inputs: two 40-minute sessions a week, step cards, work
204:   cards, case cards, paper media tools, found objects, an optional shared device per team. Activities: technique
205:   taught in steps, works studied with their setting, planning and producing in roles with a critique and a revision,
206:   a showing or screening each unit. Outputs: a portfolio of 12 pieces over the band with reflections, 12 showings,
207:   two marks a unit per student on the main look-for. Outcome claimed: met marks on the year's 18 ids and growth on
208:   the look-fors. Nothing here has been tried with students, and Curio Class makes no claim that arts study raises any
209:   other result (APPROACH "Sources").
