-for marked at the screening: every form backs the core message; MA:Pr6.1.6a, a kept card: the showing plan comparing two formats, with the tasks done to present; MA:Pr6.1.6b, a written item on the second check day: how the screening went and one improvement, with evidence from the audience; MA:Cn10.1.6a, a kept card: the source log, each source judged for quality and credited, research included; MA:Cn10.1.6b, a written item on the second check day: how one unseen public domain photograph took on a new meaning when it was used later. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a format chosen by evaluation (grade 7). Tools: the showing plan, the source log, a work viewer, Curio Class's own; public domain archive pages opened by the teacher. Free path: a paper photo-essay wall and a live-voiced picture sequence. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in the year because the screening joins the year's making, framing and sound into one piece; the screening-plan and source-log models are retrieved here, where first needed. Its screening and written check end the year's units before the year-end check in week 32 | Candidate: an archive set from outside the United States on a historical event, for range | Hine photographed real children, named in his captions: the unit studies them with respect and their setting, and no student plays a child worker. Bey's project remembers the four girls killed and two boys killed that day in Birmingham, Alabama, 15 September 1963: the unit tells the event in one plain sentence from the Birmingham Civil Rights Institute's and the museum's published words, read first by the sensitivity agent, shows no image of the bombing, and gives no role play. A student's chosen event: no family or personal history is required, and no event of violence to people is chosen without the teacher's check. Health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: screen breaks, volume low, recording only with the family's yes, family yes for any showing outside class. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
