): the child handles the light or the puppet, makes one choice and works with the partner; MA:Pr5.1.Kb, a look-for at the same screening: the child performs one part (moves the puppet or makes the sound); MA:Pr5.1.Kc, a talk item (check day 2): one thing the flashlight or a cutout taught. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: changing a finished piece (unit 3), naming roles in a showing (unit 4). Tools: step cards, the storyboard card, the light-and-shadow card, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal-box card, a borrowed flashlight or a window's daylight on a paper screen. Once-a-week path: 11 sessions | After unit 1 because shadow play needs the tool rules (two hands, set down, the light never in eyes) and play with a tool needs the habit of looking at what a piece shows. Before unit 3 because the kamishibai piece is made from a storyboard plan with the tool skills built here. The storyboard and production-roles models start here. Longest kindergarten unit: five ids, two techniques and the first partner work | Candidate: a doubled parent-and-child version of the partner shadow piece for the home room | Hazards and controls: flashlights low-power, never shone in eyes, no flashing on and off; the screen on a steady stand; blunt-tip scissors, card cut by an adult. Partner work: a child who finds sharing hard works the light while the partner moves the puppet, by turns. Prince Achmed draws on the Arabian Nights tales and shows its people and places in a 1920s European picture of "the East": the unit shows only the flying horse and the silhouette craft, and the sensitivity agent reads the copy's page and a film archive's notes first. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 3 | K | Make it, change it. *What can our picture-card story say, and what happens when we change one thing?* | 10 | ma-k-make; ma-k-judge | MA:Cr3.1.Ka; MA:Cr3.1.Kb; MA:Re8.1.K; MA:Re9.1.K | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9); connecting practiced through the kamishibai's setting (review). Works: kamishibai, the Japanese paper theater in which a street storyteller slid picture cards through a small wooden stage and told the story (Japan, from about 1930), a children's story set from a museum's or library's open collection (the unit plan picks it and records its rights line); Anna Atkins, Photographs of British Algae: Cyanotype Impressions (England, 1843 to 1853, public domain, a library's open digital copy), the same kind of plant shown again and again, each print laid out a little differently | MA:Cr3.1.Ka: create and capture a three-card picture story that says something (a pet, the weather, an invented animal), each card drawn from what the viewfinder frames. MA:Cr3.1.Kb: change one thing (a color, what is in the frame, the order of two cards) and share both versions with the class. MA:Re8.1.K: with help, share what you notice about different media pieces. MA:Re9.1.K: share what looks appealing in a piece and one thing you might change (the critique card: I notice, I like, I would change). Technique: the cereal-box kamishibai stage, cards slid in order and told aloud by the child or a partner. Words: make, change, card, notice, like, version. Showing: both versions told to a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr3.1.Ka, a guide look-for on the kept three cards: three cards in order that say one thing; MA:Cr3.1.Kb, a guide look-for on the kept before-and-after card: the first card and the changed card side by side, the change told to the teacher at the showing and noted on the record sheet; MA:Re8.1.K, a talk item: one thing noticed about each of two unseen pieces (an open-licensed photograph and a Curio Class card); MA:Re9.1.K, a talk item: on an unseen piece, what looks appealing and one change. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: pattern and repetition (grade 1). Tools: the kamishibai stage template, picture cards, the critique card, the before-and-after card, Curio Class's own. Free path: a cereal box and scrap paper. Once-a-week path: 4 sessions | After unit 2 because the story is made from a storyboard plan and with the tool habits; judging needs unit 1's noticing of parts. Before unit 4 because the screening joins a piece the child has already made and changed. The revision-log and critique-protocol models start here. Short because the plan, the framing and the tool skills are already built | Candidate: a second picture-card form from Africa or the Americas beside kamishibai, for range | Hazards and controls: cereal boxes cut by an adult; blunt-tip scissors and glue sticks; hands washed. Kamishibai was also used for wartime messages in 1930s and 1940s Japan: the unit picks a children's story set, and the sensitivity agent reads the holding collection's page and a Japanese museum's or cultural body's page on kamishibai first; children make their own stories in the form and copy no set's pictures. Critique talks about the work, never the maker. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 4 | K | Our class screening. *How can we join pictures and a dance into one show, and how did showing it feel?* | 10 | ma-k-present; ma-k-sources | MA:Pr4.1.K; MA:Pr6.1.Ka; MA:Pr6.1.Kb; MA:Cn10.1.Ka; MA:Cn10.1.Kb | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10); creating practiced on the strip (review). Works: Annabelle Serpentine Dance (United States, 1895, filmed at Thomas Edison's studio and colored by hand, the Library of Congress, public domain), a dance joined with film and color; McLaren and Lambart's Begone Dull Care from unit 2, revisited: paint and music joined | MA:Pr4.1.K: with help, join two art forms into one piece: a paper film strip (a long strip of drawn pictures pulled through a box window) with a class movement made to it. MA:Pr6.1.Ka: with help, name the roles (puller, mover, sound maker, usher) and the place where the piece is shown, on the screening-plan card. MA:Pr6.1.Kb: with help, share reactions to the screening (how showing felt, what the audience did). MA:Cn10.1.Ka: use own experiences and choices in the piece (the strip shows something the child did or likes, or a story character's, marked the same). MA:Cn10.1.Kb: share a memorable media moment (a song, a picture book read aloud, a picture), with a class-shared moment (unit 2's shadow screening) offered beside it and marked the same. Technique: the pull-through film strip in a box window, pulled at a steady pace to a movement or a sound. Words: join, dance, screen, role, show, feel. Showing: the class screening for families, with each family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.K, a look-for at the screening (check day 1): pictures and movement joined in one piece; MA:Pr6.1.Ka, a kept card made on the unit's last making day: the screening-plan card with the roles and the place pictured, scored with the piece; MA:Cn10.1.Ka, a guide look-for on the kept strip: a picture from the child's own experience or choice (or the story character's, marked the same); MA:Pr6.1.Kb, a talk item (check day 2): how showing felt and one thing the audience did; MA:Cn10.1.Kb, a talk item (check day 2): one memorable media moment, the child's own or the class-shared one, marked the same. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: showing conditions (grade 1). Tools: the film-strip box template, the screening-plan card, Curio Class's own. Free path: a cereal box and taped paper strips. Once-a-week path: 4 sessions | Last in the year because the screening joins the year's framing, plan and change skills and ends the year. The year-end check sits before it (week 27), outside the core checks of weeks 29 to 32; this unit's screening in weeks 28 to 32 is its own assessment. The screening-plan and source-log models start here | Candidate: the found-sound track written as the full equal of the movement version | Leave-out: a school may leave out the movement (STANDARD.md 5); a found-sound track joined to the strip keeps every id met. The movement is the class's own, never an imitation of a tradition's dance. Hazards and controls: a clear floor with room to move arms out, no jumping from heights, no partner contact; strips pulled by hand, no sharp box edges. Memorable media moments are invited, never required: no child is asked what the family watches, and home media is never judged. No child is filmed; a family's yes before any showing outside class, under the child's chosen name. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 5 | 1 | Media that links people. *How do pictures and films link us to people we know, and how do two pieces feel different?* | 18 | ma-g1-read; ma-g1-society | MA:Re7.1.1a; MA:Re7.1.1b; MA:Cn11.1.1a; MA:Cn11.1.1b | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11). Works: Horace Poolaw (Kiowa, 1906 to 1984), photographs of Kiowa family and community life in and around Anadarko, Oklahoma (about 1925 to 1955), from the Smithsonian's National Museum of the American Indian (its 2014 exhibition "For a Love of His People"), described and linked; Louis Lumière, Repas de bébé (Baby's Meal) (France, 1895, public domain), a family at breakfast filmed: a still and a moving picture of family life | Retrieval in day 2's opener: noticing parts and messages (ma-k-read) and safe, polite tool use (ma-k-society). MA:Re7.1.1a: name what media pieces contain (people, a place, things, a sound) and the messages they carry. MA:Re7.1.1b: with help, name how two pieces create different experiences (a still photograph of a family against a short film of one). MA:Cn11.1.1a: discuss media in daily life and how it links people you know (a picture sent to a grandparent, a call, a class newsletter), with a story character's or an invented family's link offered beside the child's own and marked the same. MA:Cn11.1.1b: use tools and spaces by the rules, with safety and fair play: the class media rules the children help write (ask before a picture of anyone; a classmate only with their yes and the family's; the device shared by the turn card). Technique: far, middle and close shots with the viewfinder, drawn in three frames. Words: contents, message, far, close, link, fair. Showing: a "media links us" wall of three-frame drawings of places and objects. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.1a, a talk item: on an unseen open-licensed photograph, what it contains and its message; MA:Re7.1.1b, a talk item: an unseen still and an unseen short film clip of one subject, how each feels different; MA:Cn11.1.1a, a guide look-for on the kept "media links us" drawing: a media link between people (the child's own, invented or a story character's, marked the same), told to the teacher; MA:Cn11.1.1b, a table item: with the viewfinder or the device from the tray, the child shows the turn card and the ask-first rule in action and names one class rule. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: media in cultural life (grade 2). Tools: the three-frame card, the class rules card, work cards, step cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: viewfinders from year K, scrap paper. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First in grade 1 because it reopens the tools and the rules after the summer and moves the looking routine from parts to contents and messages. Both kindergarten models (look-describe-interpret, media-ethics-check) are retrieved here, where they are first needed this year. The far, middle and close shots taught here are what unit 6's sketches in order draw | Candidate: a third photograph of family life by a living maker from Asia or Africa, described and linked, for range | Poolaw photographed his own Kiowa family and community: the sensitivity agent studies the National Museum of the American Indian's pages and a Kiowa-run source first; the unit picks everyday-life photographs, not ceremonies, and no child imitates dress or regalia. Families vary: "people you know" includes classmates, neighbors and an invented family. Hazards and controls: shared devices by the turn card, wiped and handed back; screen breaks; no picture of a child without a yes. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 6 | 1 | Pictures in order. *How do media makers plan a piece in pictures, and what jobs does a team need?* | 22 | ma-g1-ideas; ma-g1-craft | MA:Cr1.1.1; MA:Cr2.1.1; MA:Pr5.1.1a; MA:Pr5.1.1b; MA:Pr5.1.1c | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Émile Cohl, Fantasmagorie (France, 1908, public domain), a film made from hundreds of line drawings in order; Reiniger's Prince Achmed from year K, revisited: the same moving-picture idea made with cut paper instead of line | Retrieval in day 2's opener: play and a first plan (ma-k-ideas) and a tool used with a partner (ma-k-craft). MA:Cr1.1.1: show an idea in three sketches in order and a small model. MA:Cr2.1.1: with help, build a mockup of one named idea: a four-to-eight page flip book. MA:Pr5.1.1a: describe and show a role (planner, drawer, page holder, sound helper) and its skills (planning, tool steps, teamwork). MA:Pr5.1.1b: show basic creativity by changing up a technique (the flip book drawn again bigger, in color, or with a faster move). MA:Pr5.1.1c: try varied uses of a tool (the flip book flipped slow and fast, the viewfinder close and far) and show classmates what you found. Technique: the flip book, small changes from page to page. Words: sketch, order, mockup, flip book, role, try. Showing: team flip books shown to a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr1.1.1, a guide look-for on the kept sketch page: three sketches in order and a small model; MA:Cr2.1.1, a guide look-for on the kept flip-book mockup: built from the named idea on the sketch page; MA:Pr5.1.1a, a talk item: with the role card, the child names the role and shows and tells one of its steps; MA:Pr5.1.1b, a guide look-for on the two kept flip books: the first version and the changed one; MA:Pr5.1.1c, a table item: with a flip book or viewfinder from the tray, the child shows one varied use and tells what it found. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: brainstorming many ideas (grade 2). Tools: the sketch-in-order card, flip-book template, role cards, Curio Class's own; a frame-sequence viewer or its printed flip book. Free path: scrap paper stapled or clipped. Once-a-week path: 11 sessions | After unit 5 because sketches in order draw its far, middle and close shots. Before unit 7, whose pattern and repetition need pages in order. Longest grade 1 unit: five ids, the flip book and the first named roles | Candidate: an animated work by a living maker from Africa or Asia, described and linked, for range | Team work: every role has a seated, no-drawing and no-speaking version; a child may work beside the team with a doubled task. Prince Achmed: as unit 2, only the flying horse and the craft are shown. Staples or clips by an adult; blunt scissors. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 7 | 1 | Patterns that repeat. *How do patterns and repeats help a media piece, and which part works best for its viewers?* | 10 | ma-g1-make; ma-g1-judge | MA:Cr3.1.1a; MA:Cr3.1.1b; MA:Re8.1.1; MA:Re9.1.1 | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: J.D. 'Okhai Ojeikere, the Hairstyles series of photographs (Nigeria, from 1968), described and linked to a holding museum's page; Muybridge's The Horse in Motion from year K, revisited: a repeat of frames | MA:Cr3.1.1a: make and record content (frames drawn through the viewfinder, or photographs of objects on a shared device), assemble them into a six-frame strip, and spot pattern and repetition (a repeated frame, close, far, close). MA:Cr3.1.1b: try a change, notice what it does, and use it to finish. MA:Re8.1.1: with help, identify what different pieces mean. MA:Re9.1.1: name the part of a piece that works best and a possible change, thinking of its viewers. Technique: a six-frame sequence with a repeat, laid out as a strip. Words: pattern, repeat, record, effect, meaning, viewer. Showing: the strips shown to the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr3.1.1a, a guide look-for on the kept strip: six frames assembled with a repeat or pattern, which the child has circled on the last making day; MA:Cr3.1.1b, a guide look-for on the kept before-and-after card: one change tried and used to finish, and what it did, dictated or drawn; MA:Re8.1.1, a talk item: what an unseen piece means; MA:Re9.1.1, a talk item: the part of an unseen piece that works best and a change for its viewers. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: placing content to draw attention (grade 2). Tools: the six-frame strip card, the before-and-after card, the critique card, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil frames on scrap paper. Once-a-week path: 4 sessions | After unit 6 because a strip with a pattern needs pages in order and a team's roles; meaning and viewers build on year K's noticing. Short because the order, framing and roles are already built | Candidate: a pattern-based piece by a living maker from Asia or the Americas, described and linked | Ojeikere photographed Nigerian women's hairstyles: the sensitivity agent reads the holding museum's page and published accounts of the series first; the unit studies pattern in the photographs, and no child is asked about, photographed for or compared by their own hair or body (APPROACH "Sensitive ground"). Critique talks about the work, never the maker. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 8 | 1 | A picture-book tale. *How can we mix a story, pictures and sound into one piece, and what do we bring to it from our own lives?* | 10 | ma-g1-present; ma-g1-sources | MA:Pr4.1.1; MA:Pr6.1.1a; MA:Pr6.1.1b; MA:Cn10.1.1a; MA:Cn10.1.1b | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10); creating practiced (review). Works: Yuyi Morales, Viva Frida (2014; Morales born 1968 in Xalapa, Mexico; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a picture book made from puppets she built and photographed in small sets, joined with painting, about the painter Frida Kahlo, described and linked to the maker's own page; kamishibai from year K, revisited: a tale told in pictures and voice | MA:Pr4.1.1: mix a subject, drawings and a sound into one piece: a picture-book tale on paper-stage cards or a four-page book, with a sound made at each page turn. MA:Pr6.1.1a: with help, talk about the showing conditions (where, the light, can everyone see and hear) and do one task in the showing. MA:Pr6.1.1b: with help, discuss how presenting felt. MA:Cn10.1.1a: draw on own experiences, interests and an example when making (Morales drew on her love of Kahlo's art; the child names an interest and an example of their own). MA:Cn10.1.1b: share a meaningful media experience. Technique: a sound made on cue at a page turn (voice, a found-object shaker, a tapped box). Words: mix, tale, sound, cue, conditions, example. Showing: the class picture-book tales, shown to families. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.1, a look-for at the showing (check day 1): subject, drawings and a sound joined; MA:Pr6.1.1a, a look-for at the showing: the child's one task done, with the kept screening-plan card made on the last making day naming two conditions; MA:Cn10.1.1a, a guide look-for on the kept tale and its source card: an interest of the child's own and a named example; MA:Pr6.1.1b, a talk item (check day 2): how presenting felt; MA:Cn10.1.1b, a talk item (check day 2): a meaningful media experience, the child's own or the class-shared one, marked the same. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a unified narrated piece (grade 2). Tools: the screening-plan card, the source card, the sound-cue card, Curio Class's own. Free path: found objects for sound, scrap paper books. Once-a-week path: 4 sessions | Last in grade 1 because the tale joins the year's order, pattern and roles and ends the year after the year-end check (week 27); this unit's showing is its own assessment | Candidate: the sound-cue step tried with a quiet version (a visual cue card) for a child who is sensitive to noise | Kahlo: the unit tells her art, her garden and her animals as the book does; her illness and injury are named only as the book names them, and the sensitivity agent reads Morales's own published words on the book first. No child dresses as Kahlo or imitates her look (APPROACH: never by costume). Sound: shakers at low volume; hearing protection offered. Family yes for showing outside class. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 9 | 2 | Media messages. *What messages do media pieces carry, and how do they fit the life of a time, a place and a people?* | 18 | ma-g2-read; ma-g2-society | MA:Re7.1.2a; MA:Re7.1.2b; MA:Cn11.1.2a; MA:Cn11.1.2b | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11); creating practiced on the class poster (review). Works: posters made for the Works Progress Administration (United States, 1936 to 1943, the Library of Congress, public domain), such as ones asking people to visit the library or stay healthy (the unit plan picks posters free of the era's stereotypes); Wendy Red Star, Four Seasons (2006; Apsáalooke (Crow), born 1981; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), four staged photographs of the artist in Apsáalooke dress before plainly painted backdrops, made to answer older pictures of Native people, described and linked to the artist's own page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: contents and messages (ma-g1-read) and fair, rule-keeping tool use (ma-g1-society). MA:Re7.1.2a: name and describe what pieces contain and the messages they carry (a poster's picture, words and colors). MA:Re7.1.2b: name and describe how two pieces shape different experiences for the same viewer (a poster that tells you to do something; a photograph that makes you look again). MA:Cn11.1.2a: discuss how media ideas connect to daily and cultural life, and what a media message is (posters of one time and place; an artist answering old pictures of her people). MA:Cn11.1.2b: use tools and spaces by the rules, with safety and fair play, in a group poster project: turns, a credit line naming each maker, screen breaks. Technique: a one-message poster made from cut-paper shapes and a few words, framed with the viewfinder first. Words: message, poster, culture, experience, credit, fair. Showing: the class's message posters hung in the school's own hall. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.2a, a talk item: on an unseen public domain poster, its contents and its message; MA:Re7.1.2b, a talk item: two unseen pieces, how each shapes a different experience; MA:Cn11.1.2a, a guide look-for on the kept poster and its card: the message named and one place in daily or community life where it would be seen, written or dictated; MA:Cn11.1.2b, a table item: at the tray the child shows the group rules in action (the device turn, the credit line on the group poster pointed to) and names a safety rule. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: values and online behavior (grade 3). Tools: the poster layout card, the credit card, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: scrap paper and glue sticks. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First in grade 2 because it reopens the tools and rules after the summer, with a group project's fair-play and credit rules shown first; the message idea grows from grade 1's contents and messages. The poster's single message is what unit 11's placement for attention builds on | Candidate: a media message piece by a maker from Africa or Asia, described and linked | Red Star is Apsáalooke and made these photographs to answer how old pictures showed her people: the teacher says this plainly, and the sensitivity agent studies the artist's own published words and an Apsáalooke-run source first. Her dress is studied by looking only, as she presents it; no child dresses as, poses as or plays a Native person (STANDARD.md 5 leave-out line). WPA posters: the unit plan's picks are checked for the era's stereotypes. "Cultural life" tasks never ask a child to explain their own family's culture. Hazards and controls: blunt scissors, glue sticks, devices by the turn card. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 10 | 2 | Many ideas, one piece. *How many ideas can a team find for one piece, and how do we record and shape it?* | 22 | ma-g2-ideas; ma-g2-craft | MA:Cr1.1.2; MA:Cr2.1.2; MA:Pr5.1.2a; MA:Pr5.1.2b; MA:Pr5.1.2c | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Gitanjali Rao, Printed Rainbow (India, 2006; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a short animated film in which an old woman and her cat travel into the pictures on her matchbox covers, described and linked to the maker's or a film festival's page; Émile Reynaud, Pauvre Pierrot (France, 1892, public domain), painted pictures on a long band, shown by hand in his optical theater | Retrieval in day 2's opener: sketches in order (ma-g1-ideas) and named roles (ma-g1-craft). MA:Cr1.1.2: brainstorm and improvise many ideas for one piece (acted out with the body, or quick sketches). MA:Cr2.1.2: choose one idea and make a storyboard sketch and a mockup of it. MA:Pr5.1.2a: take a role that uses art, design, tech or people skills (drawer, mover, sound maker, frame holder, a director who checks that everyone has a turn). MA:Pr5.1.2b: experiment through play and trying again (a first stop-motion try, then again with smaller moves). MA:Pr5.1.2c: demonstrate and explore named tool methods for recording and shaping media: stop-motion (move a little, capture, move again) on a shared device or as drawn frames on paper, and a sound recorded and shaped (louder, softer, a longer pause) on a device or as a picture-mark sound score performed the same way twice. Technique: paper-cutout stop-motion from a storyboard. Words: brainstorm, improvise, storyboard, stop-motion, record, shape. Showing: team stop-motion pieces (device playback, or the paper frames flipped) shown to the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr1.1.2, a guide look-for on the kept brainstorm page: at least three ideas, sketched or acted and noted by the teacher on the page; MA:Cr2.1.2, a guide look-for on the kept storyboard and mockup of the chosen idea; MA:Pr5.1.2a, a talk item: with the role card, the child names the role and the skill it used; MA:Pr5.1.2b, a guide look-for on the kept first-try and second-try frames side by side: tried again with one change; MA:Pr5.1.2c, a table item: with a cutout and the device or paper frames from the tray, the child shows one named method (move, capture, move) and names it. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: testing a plan with classmates (grade 3). Tools: the brainstorm card, storyboard card, role cards, the sound-score card, Curio Class's own; a frame-sequence viewer or its printed flip book. Free path: paper cutouts, paper frames, voice and found objects for sound. Once-a-week path: 11 sessions | After unit 9 because a team's fair roles and credit were set there; many ideas grow from grade 1's single idea in order. Before unit 11, whose whole project combines the contents recorded and shaped here. Longest grade 2 unit: five ids and two recording methods | Candidate: a living stop-motion maker from Africa or the Americas, described and linked | Printed Rainbow is about an old woman living alone; the teacher names it gently as a story about imagination, and the sensitivity agent reads the maker's or festival's page first. Leave-out: acting out ideas may be left out; quick sketches keep every id met. Hazards and controls: no flashing in any stop-motion piece (no fast color flicker); screen breaks; sound at low volume; devices by the turn card. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
