FILE docs/APPROACH.md
82: ## The principles
83: 
84: They follow the order of a unit: plan, teach, practise, check, and then two that run through all of it.
85: 
86: **1. Start from what students know, and teach in an order where each idea rests on the last.**
87: New ideas are learned by connecting them to what is already in memory, and what a student already believes can
88: also mislead (How People Learn II, conclusion 5-1; Deans for Impact). These are summaries of a large body of
89: research, not a tested recipe for ordering a course. *Rule:* a section plan names the earlier standards it builds
90: on and checks them first; the scope and sequence gives "builds on" and "where it comes back" for every unit.
91: 
92: **2. Know it, do it, use it, together.**
93: Students learn why an idea works, become accurate and quick with it, and use it. None of the three waits for
94: the others (Deans for Impact; the math page gives that subject's sources). *Rule:* each subject page names the
95: kinds of day it balances, and a section plan marks every day as one of them.
96: 
97: **3. Teach new things clearly, in small steps, with models. Then take the help away.**
98: Working memory is small, and for beginners clear teaching beats partial guidance (Rosenshine 2012; Clark,
99: Kirschner and Sweller 2012, which is an argued position). Alternating worked examples with problems is rated
100: moderate (Pashler et al. 2007, recommendation 2). That rating is for math and science problems. In writing,
101: teaching strategies directly, with the teacher modelling and then handing over, is rated strong for elementary
102: grades and for grades 6 to 12 (WWC elementary and secondary writing guides). In reading, teaching comprehension
103: strategies the same way is rated strong for kindergarten to grade 3 and, in an older guide, for grades 4 to 12.
104: Each rating is for the whole practice, with modelling as one part. Modelling the close reading of a single text
105: is common practice with no rating. As skill grows the advantage of
106: examples reverses, so the help must fade. *Rule:* a section plan names who holds the model on each day and the day
107: it is withdrawn. A teach tool shows a model before it asks.
108: 
109: **4. A lesson may open with a short task. The teaching follows the same day.**
110: This is a real debate, and we do not pretend it is settled. Research does not support only student-centered or
111: only teacher-directed teaching (National Mathematics Advisory Panel, finding 23). A 2021 review of 53 studies
112: found that trying a problem before being taught helped older students understand ideas, on average, and did
113: nothing for procedures. For grades 2 to 5 the few comparisons pointed the other way: teaching first did
114: slightly better. In every comparison both groups were taught; only the order changed (Sinha and Kapur 2021). A
115: widely used open math course puts a task before the explanation from kindergarten up (Illustrative Mathematics
116: K-5), and science standards are built on starting from something to explain. *Rule:* an opening task is allowed
117: when students already have what they need to attempt it and the goal is an idea, not a procedure; in math, ten
118: minutes at most. In grades 2 to 5, teaching first is the default for anything new, unless the subject page says
119: otherwise and gives its source. A section plan says how each day opens and for how long.
120: 
121: **5. Talk and writing are part of learning.**
122: Asking students to explain why and how is rated strong (Pashler et al. 2007, recommendation 7); that supports
123: "why" questions, not every kind of discussion. For English learners, building spoken and written language into
124: content lessons is rated strong, and regular structured writing is recommended on minimal evidence (WWC English
125: learner guide). *Rule:* a section plan names two or three planned questions and the written task. A tool cannot
126: judge open writing; a teacher does, with a rubric and sample pieces on paper (FOUNDATION).
127: 
128: **6. Choose the medium for the idea: hands, paper or screen.**
129: Linking concrete and abstract forms of an idea is rated moderate; the same guide warns that an idea learned
130: only with objects can stay stuck to them, so the objects are faded out on a named day (Pashler et al. 2007,
131: recommendation 4). On screens the evidence is mixed. Practice and tutorial software has generally helped in
132: studies, and one large national study found no gain when schools used it in the ordinary way (National
133: Mathematics Advisory Panel, finding 28), so we do not count on software to teach by itself. No technology is
134: right everywhere (How People Learn II, conclusions 8-1, 8-2). In one interview study with college students,
135: people learned from changing a simulation themselves and little from watching it (Adams et al. 2008). *Rule:* a
136: tool is built only when a section plan names its job, and each use of it has one of three jobs (one tool may be used
137: for two, such as a teach tool that shows why and then checks unaided; the plan names the part, the job and the
138: minutes): **show why** (the student
139: predicts, changes something, sees what follows), **practise with feedback**, or **check unaided**. A tool does
140: not replace the teacher's explanation, hold a discussion, judge open work, stand in for real materials, or know
141: a student from one day to the next. The plan says what is done by hand, with what supplies.
142: 
143: **7. Practise over time, mixed, and from memory.**
144: Spacing is rated moderate and quizzing with feedback strong (Pashler et al. 2007, recommendations 1 and 5b); a
145: review of study techniques rated the same two highest, and mixing problem types promising but less certain
146: (Dunlosky 2013). *Rule:* a section plan says what earlier work is practised in it and where its own ideas come
147: back. How (a warm-up, a notebook, a return to a text) is set by the subject page.
148: 
149: **8. Every day leaves evidence the teacher can act on.**
150: Checking helps when the evidence changes the teaching, and feedback helps when it is about the work and the
151: next step, not a mark (Black and Wiliam 1998; Leahy et al. 2005). Feedback varies widely in effect and can harm
152: (EEF feedback report). *Rule:* a unit's end-of-unit assessment is written before its days. Each item names the common
153: wrong answers, what each shows, and what to do if many students made it. The form of the daily evidence is set
154: by the subject page: an exit question in math; the notebook and the class model in science. What FOUNDATION
155: costs us: no accounts means no live dashboard. The teacher holds the record, on score codes and a printed class
156: sheet, and that is slower.
157: 
158: **9. Every learner works on grade-level content, with supports built into the lesson.**
159: Teaching a small set of academic words in depth is rated strong (WWC English learner guide, recommendation 1).
160: Guidance for English learners asks that language support sit inside the lesson and the challenge be kept (ELSF;
161: WIDA 2020). Universal Design for Learning asks for more than one way in, more than one way to respond (CAST
162: 2024); we treat it as a design framework, not a proven method. *Rule:* every section plan fills three hooks: an
163: unaided check, English learner supports, and access. Extension goes deeper on the same standard. For each
164: readiness gap the plan gives a ten-minute reteach and the earlier-grade tool or page. Curio Class is not an
165: intervention program, and a student two or more years behind needs more than it offers.
166: 
167: **10. Motivation comes from success and purpose.**
168: Students work harder when they feel they belong, have some control and see themselves improving; rewards for
169: performing can lower interest, though findings are mixed (How People Learn II, chapter 6). Decoration that is
170: not about the idea hurts learning (Deans for Impact). *Rule:* pictures and stories in a tool serve the idea;
171: practice starts where most students succeed and climbs; feedback is about the task and what to try next, never
172: about the child.
173: 
174: ## The two rooms
175: 
176: A unit must work for a class of thirty and for one child at home with a parent. Tutoring one student or a small
177: group has unusually good evidence, but few of those studies were of parents, and the gains were smaller for
178: them (Nickow, Oreopoulos and Quan 2020). We read nothing on parents teaching their own child a full course. So
179: we claim no gain; we say the home room is worth serving well. Every unit carries a "teaching one child" note,
180: answer keys explain, and discussion questions are ones a parent can ask.

