FILE docs/reviews/arts-open-material-2026-11-16.md
1: # Arts: open material scout
2: 
3: Read 2026-11-16 (pre-work for the arts). Research notes, not legal advice. Every licence read on the publisher's
4: own page unless marked "search only". The bar is docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md: CC BY 4.0, CC BY 3.0 or public domain (or
5: CC0) can be a base; NC, SA, ND or unclear terms are reference only.
6: 
7: ## The short answer
8: 
9: **No openly licensed K to 12 arts curriculum fits as a base, in any of the five disciplines.** None was found
10: under CC BY or in the public domain. The arts are written fresh, under docs/courses/arts/STANDARD.md and the
11: approach page.
12: 
13: What is open is **material to study, not plans to follow**: museum images released as CC0, public domain sound
14: recordings, and one music theory text under CC BY. These serve "the works studied" and, for high school music,
15: some theory content.
16: 
17: ## Candidates: curricula and texts
18: 
19: | Candidate | Licence (as read) | Courses | Level | Verdict |
20: |---|---|---|---|---|
21: | Understanding Basic Music Theory (Catherine Schmidt-Jones), as hosted on LibreTexts | CC BY 3.0 on the LibreTexts page; other copies of the same book show CC BY 2.0 or CC BY-SA 4.0 | Music 6 to 8, music high school (theory) | Notation, rhythm, scales, chords | **Partial base** for theory content, from the LibreTexts copy only, with the credit line it asks for. Check the licence again on the exact chapter used. |
22: | Open Music Theory (v1 and v2) | CC BY-SA 4.0 (search only) | Music high school | College theory | Reference only (SA). |
23: | Kennedy Center arts education lessons (archived) | Copyright the Kennedy Center; educational use stated, no open licence | All five | K to 12 lessons | Reference only. |
24: | Smarthistory | CC BY-NC-SA (from memory, not read at source) | Visual arts | Art history essays and videos | Reference only (NC, SA). |
25: | State arts curriculum frameworks | No CC BY K to 12 arts curriculum found; one state's arts standards site shows a general open notice "except where noted", not read through | All | Standards, not units | None fits. |
26: 
27: ## Candidates: works to study (images and recordings)
28: 
29: These are not curricula. They are where a unit's works studied can come from, when the work itself is old enough
30: or released.
31: 
32: | Source | Terms (as read) | Use |
33: |---|---|---|
34: | Art Institute of Chicago open access images | CC0 for public domain works (its open access page, search only) | Visual arts works studied |
35: | The Metropolitan Museum of Art open access | CC0 for public domain works (search only; the page returned an error) | Visual arts works studied |
36: | Smithsonian open access | CC0 for released items (search only; the page refused the fetch) | Visual arts, music, media arts |
37: | Library of Congress sound recordings (National Jukebox and others) | Recordings first published before 1923 entered the US public domain in 2022 (search only) | Music works studied, dance music |
38: | Library of Congress photographs and films | Many items have no known restrictions; each item states its own rights | Media arts, theater, visual arts |
39: 
40: Each image or recording used is checked on its own item page at the time it is used: an institution can release
41: some items and not others.
42: 
43: ## What the scout does not settle
44: 
45: - Dance and theater have no open recordings of performances at scale. Units in those disciplines describe works
46:   in our own words and point to them; they do not embed.
47: - Living artists' works are not open. A unit that studies one names it and links to the artist's or museum's own
48:   page, never copies it.

