ma-g6-present ma-g5-present => ma-g7-present screening-plan => screening-plan Blend several forms in one piece to back its core message, analyze where and how it could be shown, do varied tasks to present and share it, and analyze how the showing went. What changes: formats are analyzed against a core message, and a written review replaces a talk. Assessed by a finished piece, a showing plan and a written review that ties each format to the core message, scored on a rubric. Teacher note: students show work only in class or the school's own space, and a showing outside class needs each family's yes. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device.
ma-g6-read ma-g5-read => ma-g7-read look-describe-interpret => look-describe-interpret Write an analysis of how a piece's parts build its meaning, with a reason from the piece, and how its techniques and styles steer an audience. What changes: from naming parts to explaining how a choice steers a viewer, with a reason from the piece. Assessed by a written analysis with evidence from the piece scored on a rubric for evidence and reasoning.
ma-g6-judge ma-g5-judge => ma-g7-judge critique-protocol => critique-protocol Critique pieces' intent with given criteria, then choose criteria of the student's own to judge a piece and its production steps, with feedback. What changes: criteria are given first and then chosen by the student. Assessed by a written critique that uses given criteria first and chosen criteria second, scored on a rubric for criteria, evidence and the use of feedback. Teacher note: critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class.
ma-g6-society ma-g5-society => ma-g7-society media-ethics-check => media-ethics-check Make a short piece on how media ties to identity, history, entertainment and community, and credit every source in it, checked against the class copyright and ethics card. What changes: identity joins the investigation and copyright becomes a rule for the student's own media use, where grade 5 stopped at advertising and history. Assessed by a written or made investigation with a media-ethics check, scored on a rubric for evidence, ethics and a clear claim. Teacher note: Use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device.
ma-g6-sources ma-g5-sources => ma-g7-sources source-log => source-log Find, weigh and draw on sources, including research, to make media, and show how media gives events new meaning. What changes: sources are weighed for quality and history becomes the setting, where grade 5 only gathered them. Assessed by a made piece with a source log and a written account, scored on a rubric for source quality, credit and meaning. Teacher note: use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on.

